window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2015-12-2", "source": "2015年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part,  you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay based on the picture below. You should focus on the impact of social networking websites on reading. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "As is vividly depicted in the picture, a man is talking proudly to a woman, his eyes wide open, claiming that he\nreads about three hours every day and that his favorite book is Facebook. Simple as it is, the drawing conveys a\nthought-provoking message: for many people today, so-called reading has quietly been replaced by scrolling\nthrough social networking websites.\nThe implication behind this cartoon deserves our serious attention. On one hand, social platforms do bring us\nfragmented information, giving us the illusion that we are reading a great deal. On the other hand, such\nfragmented reading rarely leaves anything solid in our minds. Constant notifications keep shortening our\nattention span, so that few of us can sit down and finish a whole book. In this sense, what the man calls reading\nis far from genuine, in-depth reading.\nIn my view, social networking websites are a double-edged sword rather than a substitute for books. We should\nmake use of them to gather information, yet consciously reserve time for serious reading that truly nourishes\nthe mind. Only by striking such a balance can we enjoy the convenience of the digital age without losing the\ndepth that classic books offer."}, "translation": {"prompt": "在帮助国际社会于2030年前消除极端贫困过程中，中国正扮演着越来越重要的角色。 自20世纪70年代末实施改革开放以来,中国已使多达四亿人摆脱了贫困。在未来五年中，中国将向其他发展中国家在减少贫困、发展教育、农业现代化、环境保护和医疗保健等方面提供援助。 中国在减少贫困方面取得了显著进步,并在促进经济增长方面做出了不懈努力,这将鼓励其他贫困国家应对自身发展中的挑战。在寻求具有自身特色的发展道路时,这些国家可以借鉴中国的经验。", "sample": "In helping the international community eliminate extreme poverty by 2030, China is playing an increasingly important role. Since the implementation of the reform and opening-up in the late 1970s, China has lifted as many as 400 million people out of poverty. In the next five years, China will provide assistance to other developing countries in such fields as poverty reduction, education development, agricultural modernization, environmental protection and health care. China has made remarkable progress in poverty reduction and unremitting efforts in promoting economic growth, which will encourage other poor countries to cope with the challenges in their own development. When seeking a development path with their own characteristics, these countries can draw on China's experience."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2015-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It seems to be a law in the technology industry that leading companies eventually lose their positions, often quickly and brutally. Mobile phone champion Nokia, one of Europe's biggest technology success stories, was no  __26__  , losing its market share in just a few years.\nIn 2007, Nokia accounted for more than 40% of mobile phone sales  __27__  . But consumers' preferences were already  __28__  toward touch-screen smartphones. With the introduction of Apple's iPhone in the middle of that year, Nokia's market share  __29__  rapidly and revenue plunged. By the end of 2013, Nokia had sold its phone business to Microsoft.\nWhat sealed Nokia's fate was a series of decisions made by Stephen Elop in his position as CEO, which he  __30__  in October 2010. Each day that Elop spent in charge of Nokia, the company's market value declined by $23 million, making him, by the numbers, one of the worst CEOs in history.\nBut Elop was not the only person at  __31__  . Nokia's board resisted change, making it impossible for the company to adapt to rapid shifts in the industry. Most  __32__  , Jorma Ollila, who had led Nokia's transition from an industrial company to a technology giant, was too fascinated by the company's  __33__  success to recognize the change that was needed to sustain its competitiveness.\nThe company also embarked on a  __34__  cost-cutting program, which included the elimination of thousands of jobs. This contributed to the  __35__  of the company's once-spirited culture, which had motivated employees to take risks and make miracles. Good leaders left the company, taking Nokia's sense of vision and direction with them. Not surprisingly, much of Nokia's most valuable design and programming talent left as well.", "matchIntro": "First-Generation College-Goers: Unprepared and Behind", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "When Nijay Williams entered college last fall as a first-generation student and Jamaican immigrant, he was academically unprepared for the rigors of higher education. Like many first-generation students, he enrolled in a medium-sized state university many of his high school peers were also attending, received a Pell Grant, and took out some small federal loans to cover other costs. Given the high price of room and board and the closeness of the school to his family, he chose to live at home and worked between 30 and 40 hours a week while taking a full class schedule."}, {"label": "B", "text": "What Nijay didn't realize about his school—Tennessee State University—was its frighteningly low graduation rate a mere 29 percent for its first-generation students. At the end of his first year, Nijay lost his Pell Grant of over $5,000 after narrowly missing the 2.0 GPA cut-off, making it impossible for him to continue paying for school."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Nijay represents a large and growing group of Americans: first-generation college students who enter school unprepared or behind. To make matters worse, these schools are ill-equipped to graduate these students—young adults who face specific challenges and obstacles. They typically carry financial burdens that outweigh those of their peers, are more likely to work while attending school, and often require significant academic remediation(补习)."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Matt Rubinoff directs I'm First, a nonprofit organization launched last October to reach out to this specific population of students. He hopes to distribute this information and help prospective college-goers find the best post-secondary fit. And while Rubinoff believes there are a good number of four-year schools that truly care about these students and set aside significant resources and programs for them, he says that number isn't high enough."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"It's not only the selective and elite institutions that provide those opportunities for a small subset of this population,\" Rubinoff said, adding that a majority of first-generation undergraduates tend toward options such as online programs, two-year colleges, and commuter state schools. \"Unfortunately, there tends to be a lack of information and support to help students think bigger and broader.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Despite this problem, many students are still drawn to these institutions—and two-year schools in particular. As a former high school teacher, I saw students choose familiar, cheaper options year after year. Instead of skipping out on higher education altogether, they chose community colleges or state schools with low bars for admittance."}, {"label": "G", "text": "\"They underestimate themselves when selecting a university,\" said Dave Jarrat, a marketing executive for Inside Track, a for-profit organization that specializes in coaching low-income students and supporting colleges in order to help students thrive. \"The reality of it is that a lot of low-income kids could be going to elite universities on a full ride scholarship and don't even realize it.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "\"Many students are coming from a situation where no one around them has the experience of successfully completing higher education, so they are coming in questioning themselves and their college worthiness,\" Jarrat continued. That helps explain why, as I'm First's Rubinoff indicated, the schools to which these students end up resorting can end up being some of the poorest matches for them. The University of Tennessee in Knoxville offers one example of this dilemma. A flagship university in the South, the school graduates just 16 percent of its first-generation students, despite its overall graduation rate of 71 percent. Located only a few hours apart, the University of Tennessee and Tennessee State are worth comparing. Tennessee State's overall graduation rate is a tiny 39 percent, but at least it has a smaller gap between the outcomes for first-generation students and those of their peers."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Still, the University of Tennessee deserves credit for being transparent. Many large institutions keep this kind of data secret—or at least make it incredibly difficult to find. The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, for instance, admits only that the graduation rate for its first-generation pupils is \"much lower\" than the percentage of all students who graduate within four years (81 percent)."}, {"label": "J", "text": "It is actually quite difficult to find reliable statistics on the issue for many schools. Higher education institutions are, under federal law, required to report graduation rates, but these reports typically only include Pell recipient numbers—not necessarily rates specific to first-generation students. Other initiatives fail to break down the data, too. Imagine how intimidating it can be for prospective students unfamiliar with the complexities of higher education to navigate this kind of information and then identify which schools are the best fit."}, {"label": "K", "text": "It was this lack of information that prompted the launch of I'm First in 2013, originally as an arm of its umbrella organization, the Center For Student Opportunity. \"If we can help to direct students to more of these types of campuses and help students to understand them to be realistic and accessible places, have them apply to these schools at greater frequency and ultimately get in and enroll, we are going to raise the success rate,\" Rubinoff said, citing a variety of colleges ranging from large state institutions to smaller private schools."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Chelsea Jones, who now directs student programming at I'm First, was a first-generation college student at Howard. Like other students new to the intimidating higher-education world, she often struggled on her path to college. \"There wasn't really a college-bound culture at my high school,\" she said. \"I wanted to go to college but I didn't really know the process.\" Jones became involved with a college-access program through Princeton University in high school. Now, she attributes much of her understanding of college to that: \"But once I got to campus, it was a completely different ball game that no one really prepared me for.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "She was fortunate, though. Howard, a well-regarded historically black college, had an array of resources for its first-generation students, including matching kids with counselors, connecting first-generation students to one another, and TRIO, a national program that supported 200 students on Howard's campus. Still, Jones represents a small percentage of first-generation students who are able to gain entry into more elite universities, which are often known for robust financial aid packages and remarkably high graduation rates for first-generation students. (Harvard, for example, boasts a six-year graduation rate for underrepresented minority groups of 98 percent)."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Christian Vazquez, a first-generation Yale graduate, is another exception, his success story setting him far apart from students such as Nijay. \"There is a lot of support at Yale, to an extent, after a while, there is too much support,\" he said, half-joking about the countless resources available at the school. Students are placed in small groups with counselors (trained seniors on campus); they have access to cultural and ethnic affinity(联系) groups, tutoring centers and also have a summer orientation specifically for first-generation students (the latter being one of the most common programs for students)."}, {"label": "O", "text": "\"Our support structure was more like: 'You are going to get through Yale; you are going to do well,'\" he said, hinting at mentors(导师), staff, and professors who all provided significant support for students who lacked confidence about \"belonging\" at such a top institution."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Saying they can no longer ignore the rising price of health care, some of the most influential medical groups in the nation are recommending that doctors weigh the costs, not just the effectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about patient care.\nThe shift, little noticed outside the medical establishment but already controversial inside it, suggests that doctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively about individual patients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent.\nIn practical terms, the new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one drug over another for cost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment—at the end of life, for example—is too expensive. In the extreme, some critics have said that making treatment decisions based on cost is a form of rationing.\nTraditionally, guidelines have heavily influenced the practice of medicine, and the latest ones are expected to make doctors more conscious of the economic consequences of their decisions, even though there's no obligation to follow them. Medical society guidelines are also used by insurance companies to help determine reimbursement(报销) policies.\nSome doctors see a potential conflict in trying to be both providers of patient care and financial overseers. \"There should be forces in society who should be concerned about the budget, but they shouldn't be functioning simultaneously as doctors,\" said Dr. Martin Samuels at a Boston hospital. He said doctors risked losing the trust of patients if they told patients, \"I'm not going to do what I think is best for you because I think it's bad for the healthcare budget in Massachusetts.\"\nDoctors can face some grim trade-offs. Studies have shown, for example, that two drugs are about equally effective in treating macular degeneration, an eye disease. But one costs $50 a dose and the other close to $2,000. Medicare could save hundreds of millions of dollars a year if everyone used the cheaper drug, Avastin, instead of the costlier one, Lucentis.\nBut the Food and Drug Administration has not approved Avastin for use in the eye, and using it rather than the alternative, Lucentis, might carry an additional, although slight, safety risk. Should doctors consider Medicare's budget in deciding what to use?\n\"I think ethically(在道德层面上) we are just worried about the patient in front of us and not trying to save money for the insurance industry or society as a whole,\" said Dr. Donald Jensen.\nStill, some analysts say that there's a role for doctors to play in cost analysis because not many others are doing so. \"In some ways,\" said Dr. Daniel Sulmasy, \"it represents a failure of wider society to take up the issue.\"", "47": "Saying they can no longer ignore the rising price of health care, some of the most influential medical groups in the nation are recommending that doctors weigh the costs, not just the effectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about patient care.\nThe shift, little noticed outside the medical establishment but already controversial inside it, suggests that doctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively about individual patients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent.\nIn practical terms, the new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one drug over another for cost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment—at the end of life, for example—is too expensive. In the extreme, some critics have said that making treatment decisions based on cost is a form of rationing.\nTraditionally, guidelines have heavily influenced the practice of medicine, and the latest ones are expected to make doctors more conscious of the economic consequences of their decisions, even though there's no obligation to follow them. Medical society guidelines are also used by insurance companies to help determine reimbursement(报销) policies.\nSome doctors see a potential conflict in trying to be both providers of patient care and financial overseers. \"There should be forces in society who should be concerned about the budget, but they shouldn't be functioning simultaneously as doctors,\" said Dr. Martin Samuels at a Boston hospital. He said doctors risked losing the trust of patients if they told patients, \"I'm not going to do what I think is best for you because I think it's bad for the healthcare budget in Massachusetts.\"\nDoctors can face some grim trade-offs. Studies have shown, for example, that two drugs are about equally effective in treating macular degeneration, an eye disease. But one costs $50 a dose and the other close to $2,000. Medicare could save hundreds of millions of dollars a year if everyone used the cheaper drug, Avastin, instead of the costlier one, Lucentis.\nBut the Food and Drug Administration has not approved Avastin for use in the eye, and using it rather than the alternative, Lucentis, might carry an additional, although slight, safety risk. Should doctors consider Medicare's budget in deciding what to use?\n\"I think ethically(在道德层面上) we are just worried about the patient in front of us and not trying to save money for the insurance industry or society as a whole,\" said Dr. Donald Jensen.\nStill, some analysts say that there's a role for doctors to play in cost analysis because not many others are doing so. \"In some ways,\" said Dr. Daniel Sulmasy, \"it represents a failure of wider society to take up the issue.\"", "48": "Saying they can no longer ignore the rising price of health care, some of the most influential medical groups in the nation are recommending that doctors weigh the costs, not just the effectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about patient care.\nThe shift, little noticed outside the medical establishment but already controversial inside it, suggests that doctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively about individual patients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent.\nIn practical terms, the new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one drug over another for cost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment—at the end of life, for example—is too expensive. In the extreme, some critics have said that making treatment decisions based on cost is a form of rationing.\nTraditionally, guidelines have heavily influenced the practice of medicine, and the latest ones are expected to make doctors more conscious of the economic consequences of their decisions, even though there's no obligation to follow them. Medical society guidelines are also used by insurance companies to help determine reimbursement(报销) policies.\nSome doctors see a potential conflict in trying to be both providers of patient care and financial overseers. \"There should be forces in society who should be concerned about the budget, but they shouldn't be functioning simultaneously as doctors,\" said Dr. Martin Samuels at a Boston hospital. He said doctors risked losing the trust of patients if they told patients, \"I'm not going to do what I think is best for you because I think it's bad for the healthcare budget in Massachusetts.\"\nDoctors can face some grim trade-offs. Studies have shown, for example, that two drugs are about equally effective in treating macular degeneration, an eye disease. But one costs $50 a dose and the other close to $2,000. Medicare could save hundreds of millions of dollars a year if everyone used the cheaper drug, Avastin, instead of the costlier one, Lucentis.\nBut the Food and Drug Administration has not approved Avastin for use in the eye, and using it rather than the alternative, Lucentis, might carry an additional, although slight, safety risk. Should doctors consider Medicare's budget in deciding what to use?\n\"I think ethically(在道德层面上) we are just worried about the patient in front of us and not trying to save money for the insurance industry or society as a whole,\" said Dr. Donald Jensen.\nStill, some analysts say that there's a role for doctors to play in cost analysis because not many others are doing so. \"In some ways,\" said Dr. Daniel Sulmasy, \"it represents a failure of wider society to take up the issue.\"", "49": "Saying they can no longer ignore the rising price of health care, some of the most influential medical groups in the nation are recommending that doctors weigh the costs, not just the effectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about patient care.\nThe shift, little noticed outside the medical establishment but already controversial inside it, suggests that doctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively about individual patients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent.\nIn practical terms, the new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one drug over another for cost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment—at the end of life, for example—is too expensive. In the extreme, some critics have said that making treatment decisions based on cost is a form of rationing.\nTraditionally, guidelines have heavily influenced the practice of medicine, and the latest ones are expected to make doctors more conscious of the economic consequences of their decisions, even though there's no obligation to follow them. Medical society guidelines are also used by insurance companies to help determine reimbursement(报销) policies.\nSome doctors see a potential conflict in trying to be both providers of patient care and financial overseers. \"There should be forces in society who should be concerned about the budget, but they shouldn't be functioning simultaneously as doctors,\" said Dr. Martin Samuels at a Boston hospital. He said doctors risked losing the trust of patients if they told patients, \"I'm not going to do what I think is best for you because I think it's bad for the healthcare budget in Massachusetts.\"\nDoctors can face some grim trade-offs. Studies have shown, for example, that two drugs are about equally effective in treating macular degeneration, an eye disease. But one costs $50 a dose and the other close to $2,000. Medicare could save hundreds of millions of dollars a year if everyone used the cheaper drug, Avastin, instead of the costlier one, Lucentis.\nBut the Food and Drug Administration has not approved Avastin for use in the eye, and using it rather than the alternative, Lucentis, might carry an additional, although slight, safety risk. Should doctors consider Medicare's budget in deciding what to use?\n\"I think ethically(在道德层面上) we are just worried about the patient in front of us and not trying to save money for the insurance industry or society as a whole,\" said Dr. Donald Jensen.\nStill, some analysts say that there's a role for doctors to play in cost analysis because not many others are doing so. \"In some ways,\" said Dr. Daniel Sulmasy, \"it represents a failure of wider society to take up the issue.\"", "50": "Saying they can no longer ignore the rising price of health care, some of the most influential medical groups in the nation are recommending that doctors weigh the costs, not just the effectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about patient care.\nThe shift, little noticed outside the medical establishment but already controversial inside it, suggests that doctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively about individual patients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent.\nIn practical terms, the new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one drug over another for cost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment—at the end of life, for example—is too expensive. In the extreme, some critics have said that making treatment decisions based on cost is a form of rationing.\nTraditionally, guidelines have heavily influenced the practice of medicine, and the latest ones are expected to make doctors more conscious of the economic consequences of their decisions, even though there's no obligation to follow them. Medical society guidelines are also used by insurance companies to help determine reimbursement(报销) policies.\nSome doctors see a potential conflict in trying to be both providers of patient care and financial overseers. \"There should be forces in society who should be concerned about the budget, but they shouldn't be functioning simultaneously as doctors,\" said Dr. Martin Samuels at a Boston hospital. He said doctors risked losing the trust of patients if they told patients, \"I'm not going to do what I think is best for you because I think it's bad for the healthcare budget in Massachusetts.\"\nDoctors can face some grim trade-offs. Studies have shown, for example, that two drugs are about equally effective in treating macular degeneration, an eye disease. But one costs $50 a dose and the other close to $2,000. Medicare could save hundreds of millions of dollars a year if everyone used the cheaper drug, Avastin, instead of the costlier one, Lucentis.\nBut the Food and Drug Administration has not approved Avastin for use in the eye, and using it rather than the alternative, Lucentis, might carry an additional, although slight, safety risk. Should doctors consider Medicare's budget in deciding what to use?\n\"I think ethically(在道德层面上) we are just worried about the patient in front of us and not trying to save money for the insurance industry or society as a whole,\" said Dr. Donald Jensen.\nStill, some analysts say that there's a role for doctors to play in cost analysis because not many others are doing so. \"In some ways,\" said Dr. Daniel Sulmasy, \"it represents a failure of wider society to take up the issue.\"", "51": "Economic inequality is the \"defining challenge of our time,\" President Barack Obama declared in a speech last month to the Center for American Progress. Inequality is dangerous, he argued, not merely because it doesn't look good to have a large gap between the rich and the poor, but because inequality itself destroys upward mobility, making it harder for the poor to escape from poverty. \"Increased inequality and decreasing mobility pose a fundamental threat to the American Dream,\" he said.\nObama is only the most prominent public figure to declare inequality Public Enemy No.1 and the greatest threat to reducing poverty in America. A number of prominent economists have also argued that it's harder for the poor to climb the economic ladder today because the rungs(横档) in that ladder have grown farther apart.\nFor all the new attention devoted to the 1 percent, a new dataset from the Equality of Opportunity Project at Harvard and Berkeley suggests that, if we care about upward mobility overall, we're vastly exaggerating the dangers of the rich-poor gap. Inequality itself is not a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility, as sociologist Scott Winship noted in a recent article based on his analysis of this data.\nSo what factors, at the community level, do predict if poor children will move up the economic ladder as adults? What explains, for instance, why the Salt Lake City metro area is one of the 100 largest metropolitan areas most likely to lift the fortunes of the poor and the Atlanta metro area is one of the least likely?\nHarvard economist Raj Chetty has pointed to economic and racial segregation, community density, the size of a community's middle class, the quality of schools, community religiosity, and family structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility.\" Chetty finds that communities like Salt Lake City, with high levels of two-parent families and religiosity, are much more likely to see poor children get ahead than communities like Atlanta, with high levels of racial and economic segregation.\nChetty has not yet issued a comprehensive analysis of the relative predictive power of each of these factors. Based on my analyses of the data, of the factors that Chetty has highlighted, the following three seem to be most predictive of upward mobility in a given community: 1. Per-capita(人均) income growth 2. Prevalence of single mothers (where correlation is strong, but negative) 3. Per-capita local government spending\nIn other words, communities with high levels of per-capita income growth, high percentages of two-parent families, and high local government spending—which may stand for good schools—are the most likely to help poor children relive the Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story.", "52": "Economic inequality is the \"defining challenge of our time,\" President Barack Obama declared in a speech last month to the Center for American Progress. Inequality is dangerous, he argued, not merely because it doesn't look good to have a large gap between the rich and the poor, but because inequality itself destroys upward mobility, making it harder for the poor to escape from poverty. \"Increased inequality and decreasing mobility pose a fundamental threat to the American Dream,\" he said.\nObama is only the most prominent public figure to declare inequality Public Enemy No.1 and the greatest threat to reducing poverty in America. A number of prominent economists have also argued that it's harder for the poor to climb the economic ladder today because the rungs(横档) in that ladder have grown farther apart.\nFor all the new attention devoted to the 1 percent, a new dataset from the Equality of Opportunity Project at Harvard and Berkeley suggests that, if we care about upward mobility overall, we're vastly exaggerating the dangers of the rich-poor gap. Inequality itself is not a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility, as sociologist Scott Winship noted in a recent article based on his analysis of this data.\nSo what factors, at the community level, do predict if poor children will move up the economic ladder as adults? What explains, for instance, why the Salt Lake City metro area is one of the 100 largest metropolitan areas most likely to lift the fortunes of the poor and the Atlanta metro area is one of the least likely?\nHarvard economist Raj Chetty has pointed to economic and racial segregation, community density, the size of a community's middle class, the quality of schools, community religiosity, and family structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility.\" Chetty finds that communities like Salt Lake City, with high levels of two-parent families and religiosity, are much more likely to see poor children get ahead than communities like Atlanta, with high levels of racial and economic segregation.\nChetty has not yet issued a comprehensive analysis of the relative predictive power of each of these factors. Based on my analyses of the data, of the factors that Chetty has highlighted, the following three seem to be most predictive of upward mobility in a given community: 1. Per-capita(人均) income growth 2. Prevalence of single mothers (where correlation is strong, but negative) 3. Per-capita local government spending\nIn other words, communities with high levels of per-capita income growth, high percentages of two-parent families, and high local government spending—which may stand for good schools—are the most likely to help poor children relive the Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story.", "53": "Economic inequality is the \"defining challenge of our time,\" President Barack Obama declared in a speech last month to the Center for American Progress. Inequality is dangerous, he argued, not merely because it doesn't look good to have a large gap between the rich and the poor, but because inequality itself destroys upward mobility, making it harder for the poor to escape from poverty. \"Increased inequality and decreasing mobility pose a fundamental threat to the American Dream,\" he said.\nObama is only the most prominent public figure to declare inequality Public Enemy No.1 and the greatest threat to reducing poverty in America. A number of prominent economists have also argued that it's harder for the poor to climb the economic ladder today because the rungs(横档) in that ladder have grown farther apart.\nFor all the new attention devoted to the 1 percent, a new dataset from the Equality of Opportunity Project at Harvard and Berkeley suggests that, if we care about upward mobility overall, we're vastly exaggerating the dangers of the rich-poor gap. Inequality itself is not a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility, as sociologist Scott Winship noted in a recent article based on his analysis of this data.\nSo what factors, at the community level, do predict if poor children will move up the economic ladder as adults? What explains, for instance, why the Salt Lake City metro area is one of the 100 largest metropolitan areas most likely to lift the fortunes of the poor and the Atlanta metro area is one of the least likely?\nHarvard economist Raj Chetty has pointed to economic and racial segregation, community density, the size of a community's middle class, the quality of schools, community religiosity, and family structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility.\" Chetty finds that communities like Salt Lake City, with high levels of two-parent families and religiosity, are much more likely to see poor children get ahead than communities like Atlanta, with high levels of racial and economic segregation.\nChetty has not yet issued a comprehensive analysis of the relative predictive power of each of these factors. Based on my analyses of the data, of the factors that Chetty has highlighted, the following three seem to be most predictive of upward mobility in a given community: 1. Per-capita(人均) income growth 2. Prevalence of single mothers (where correlation is strong, but negative) 3. Per-capita local government spending\nIn other words, communities with high levels of per-capita income growth, high percentages of two-parent families, and high local government spending—which may stand for good schools—are the most likely to help poor children relive the Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story.", "54": "Economic inequality is the \"defining challenge of our time,\" President Barack Obama declared in a speech last month to the Center for American Progress. Inequality is dangerous, he argued, not merely because it doesn't look good to have a large gap between the rich and the poor, but because inequality itself destroys upward mobility, making it harder for the poor to escape from poverty. \"Increased inequality and decreasing mobility pose a fundamental threat to the American Dream,\" he said.\nObama is only the most prominent public figure to declare inequality Public Enemy No.1 and the greatest threat to reducing poverty in America. A number of prominent economists have also argued that it's harder for the poor to climb the economic ladder today because the rungs(横档) in that ladder have grown farther apart.\nFor all the new attention devoted to the 1 percent, a new dataset from the Equality of Opportunity Project at Harvard and Berkeley suggests that, if we care about upward mobility overall, we're vastly exaggerating the dangers of the rich-poor gap. Inequality itself is not a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility, as sociologist Scott Winship noted in a recent article based on his analysis of this data.\nSo what factors, at the community level, do predict if poor children will move up the economic ladder as adults? What explains, for instance, why the Salt Lake City metro area is one of the 100 largest metropolitan areas most likely to lift the fortunes of the poor and the Atlanta metro area is one of the least likely?\nHarvard economist Raj Chetty has pointed to economic and racial segregation, community density, the size of a community's middle class, the quality of schools, community religiosity, and family structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility.\" Chetty finds that communities like Salt Lake City, with high levels of two-parent families and religiosity, are much more likely to see poor children get ahead than communities like Atlanta, with high levels of racial and economic segregation.\nChetty has not yet issued a comprehensive analysis of the relative predictive power of each of these factors. Based on my analyses of the data, of the factors that Chetty has highlighted, the following three seem to be most predictive of upward mobility in a given community: 1. Per-capita(人均) income growth 2. Prevalence of single mothers (where correlation is strong, but negative) 3. Per-capita local government spending\nIn other words, communities with high levels of per-capita income growth, high percentages of two-parent families, and high local government spending—which may stand for good schools—are the most likely to help poor children relive the Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story.", "55": "Economic inequality is the \"defining challenge of our time,\" President Barack Obama declared in a speech last month to the Center for American Progress. Inequality is dangerous, he argued, not merely because it doesn't look good to have a large gap between the rich and the poor, but because inequality itself destroys upward mobility, making it harder for the poor to escape from poverty. \"Increased inequality and decreasing mobility pose a fundamental threat to the American Dream,\" he said.\nObama is only the most prominent public figure to declare inequality Public Enemy No.1 and the greatest threat to reducing poverty in America. A number of prominent economists have also argued that it's harder for the poor to climb the economic ladder today because the rungs(横档) in that ladder have grown farther apart.\nFor all the new attention devoted to the 1 percent, a new dataset from the Equality of Opportunity Project at Harvard and Berkeley suggests that, if we care about upward mobility overall, we're vastly exaggerating the dangers of the rich-poor gap. Inequality itself is not a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility, as sociologist Scott Winship noted in a recent article based on his analysis of this data.\nSo what factors, at the community level, do predict if poor children will move up the economic ladder as adults? What explains, for instance, why the Salt Lake City metro area is one of the 100 largest metropolitan areas most likely to lift the fortunes of the poor and the Atlanta metro area is one of the least likely?\nHarvard economist Raj Chetty has pointed to economic and racial segregation, community density, the size of a community's middle class, the quality of schools, community religiosity, and family structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility.\" Chetty finds that communities like Salt Lake City, with high levels of two-parent families and religiosity, are much more likely to see poor children get ahead than communities like Atlanta, with high levels of racial and economic segregation.\nChetty has not yet issued a comprehensive analysis of the relative predictive power of each of these factors. Based on my analyses of the data, of the factors that Chetty has highlighted, the following three seem to be most predictive of upward mobility in a given community: 1. Per-capita(人均) income growth 2. Prevalence of single mothers (where correlation is strong, but negative) 3. Per-capita local government spending\nIn other words, communities with high levels of per-capita income growth, high percentages of two-parent families, and high local government spending—which may stand for good schools—are the most likely to help poor children relive the Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story."}, "questions": [{"id": "2015-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's daily life?", "options": ["A) He cooks dinner for the family occasionally.", "B) He dines out from time to time with friends.", "C) He shares some of the household duties.", "D) He often goes back home late for dinner."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He often goes back home late for dinner.\n【定位】Susan is expecting me for dinner. I want it to be on time for a change.\n苏珊在等我回去吃晚饭。我想这次能难得准时一回。\n【信号】for a change 暗示平时相反。答案信号是 for a change（难得换个样、破例一次）。说\n『想这次准时』并加上 for a change，等于反向透露『平时往往不准时、常晚归』，这层言\n下之意正是答案落点。\n【替换】I want it to be on time for a change ↔ He often goes back home late for dinner.\n反差词反推常态\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项借用了原文反复出现的 dinner 制造熟悉感，但原文只说苏珊等他回去吃饭，从没说他\n给家里做饭，属听到 dinner 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Susan is expecting me for dinner\nB) 未提及\ndines out from time to time with friends 说他常和朋友在外吃饭，原文通篇没有任何与\n朋友外出聚餐的内容，属凭常识想象的未提及项。\nC) 未提及\nshares some of the household duties 把『准时回家吃饭』偷换成『分担家务』，原文只\n字未提他做家务，是无中生有的概念。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why did the woman come to see the man?", "options": ["A) To take him to dinner.", "B) To discuss an urgent problem.", "C) To talk about a budget plan.", "D) To pass on an important message."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To discuss an urgent problem.\n【定位】Look, I'm terribly sorry to drop in at this time on Friday, Paul. But it is rather important.\n听着，非常抱歉周五这个时间贸然来访，保罗。但这事相当重要。\n【信号】But 转折后点出重要性。答案信号是转折词 But：前半是致歉的客套，转折之后的 it is\nrather important 才是她说明来意的真意——有要紧事要谈，这才是答案落点。\n【替换】it is rather important ↔ To discuss an urgent problem.\nrather important 概括为 urgent\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项借原文里 dinner 一词，把男方『正要回家吃饭』错接成女方『来带他吃饭』，原文女\n方从未提请客吃饭，属听到 dinner 就选的原词陷阱兼张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：Susan is expecting me for dinner\nC) 偷换概念\nTo talk about a budget plan. 用『预算计划』偷换女方真正要谈的汇率风险话题，原文谈\n的是 exchange rates 与对冲，不是预算，属偷换概念。\nD) 答非所问\nTo pass on an important message. 把『有要紧事要当面商量』曲解为『来转交一条重要\n口信』，原文女方是来与男方商讨对策、不是替别人传话，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：it is rather important", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What makes the woman worry about the Indian rupee?", "options": ["A) Foreign investors are losing confidence in India's economy.", "B) Many multinational enterprises are withdrawing from India.", "C) There is a sharp increase in India's balance of payment deficit.", "D) There are wild fluctuations in the international money market."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） There is a sharp increase in India's balance of payment deficit.\n【定位】The Indian rupee has taken a fall on the foreign exchange market. You see there has been a\nsharp increase in India's balance of payment deficit.\n印度卢比在外汇市场上下跌了。你看，印度的国际收支逆差大幅上升了。\n【信号】there has been 引出根本原因。答案信号是 there has been a sharp increase：女方先说\n卢比下跌这一现象，紧接着用 there has been 引出造成下跌、令她担忧的根本原因——国\n际收支逆差大幅上升，这一句就是答案落点。\n【替换】there has been a sharp increase in India's balance of payment deficit ↔\nThere is a sharp increase in India's balance of payment deficit.\nnear-原句近乎照搬\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nForeign investors are losing confidence in India's economy. 听上去合理，但原文只提\n到 reports of unrest（动乱报道）使前景看淡，并未说『外国投资者正失去信心』，是由动\n乱一步跨到『投资者信心』的过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：the prospects for the rupee look pretty gloomy\nB) 未提及\nMany multinational enterprises are withdrawing from India. 说跨国企业纷纷撤离印\n度，原文全程没有任何企业撤资的内容，属凭常识想象的未提及项。\nD) 原词陷阱\nThere are wild fluctuations in the international money market. 借用原文 foreign\nexchange market 中 market 一词，把『卢比单向下跌』偷换成『国际货币市场剧烈波\n动』，原文说的是卢比走低、不是整个市场大幅震荡，属原词陷阱兼偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：The Indian rupee has taken a fall on the foreign exchange market", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What can we infer from the conversation?", "options": ["A) JL Motors is a company with the business of bicycle sales.", "B) The Indian rupee has been steady on the foreign exchange market.", "C) JL Motors is an Indian company with a promising prospect.", "D) The man doesn't agree to the woman's proposal."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题） JL Motors is a company with the business of bicycle sales.\n【定位】A small percentage, about 1%. And that can be built into the price of the bike.\n一个很小的比例，大约百分之一。而这部分可以算进自行车的价格里。\n【信号】顺带说出的 the price of the bike。答案信号是女方顺口说出的 built into the price of\nthe bike：她在解释成本时提到这笔费用可算进『自行车(bike)的价格』，由此可一步推出\nJL Motors 经营的是自行车（bicycle）业务，这是隐藏在细节里的推断证据。\n【替换】built into the price of the bike ↔ JL Motors is a company with the business of bicycle sales.\nbike 同义于 bicycle\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThe Indian rupee has been steady on the foreign exchange market. 说卢比稳定，恰与\n原文 The Indian rupee has taken a fall 相反，原文明说卢比下跌，故此项极性反了。\n借⽤原⽂：The Indian rupee has taken a fall on the foreign exchange market\nC) 与原文相反\nJL Motors is an Indian company with a promising prospect. 既无依据说 JL Motors 是\n印度公司，又把原文 the prospects for the rupee look pretty gloomy（前景黯淡）说成\npromising（看好），属与原文相反兼无依据拔高。\n借⽤原⽂：the prospects for the rupee look pretty gloomy\nD) 与原文相反\nThe man doesn't agree to the woman's proposal. 说男方不同意，但结尾男方明确表态\nAll right, Jane. Let's put it into action.（好的，付诸行动），其实是同意了，故此项与原\n文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Let's put it into action", "points": ["听力·长对话", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say about most people when they get into love affairs?", "options": ["A) They try to adapt to their changing roles.", "B) They form a more realistic picture of life.", "C) They may not be prepared for a lifelong relationship.", "D) They have unrealistic expectations about the other half."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have unrealistic expectations about the other half.\n【定位】People often get into love affairs because they have unrealistic expectations about somebody.\n人们常常陷入恋情，是因为他们对某个人抱有不切实际的期望。\n【信号】被问者开口第一句直给原因。女士问“爱为何消亡”，男士应答的第一句 People often get\ninto love affairs because they have unrealistic expectations about somebody. 用\nbecause 直接给出原因，这种“被问者应答 + because 因果”正是答案句的强信号，开口就\n是落点。\n【替换】unrealistic expectations about somebody ↔ unrealistic expectations about the other half\nsomebody 即恋爱里的另一半\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey try to adapt to their changing roles. 谈“适应角色转变”，男士整段只讲“想改变对方\n是错的”，从没提“适应自己变化的角色”，属凭常识脑补、原文未提。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey form a more realistic picture of life. 说人们形成“更现实”的人生图景，可男士原话\n是 unrealistic expectations（不切实际），B 把 unrealistic 偷偷反成 realistic，极性正相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：they have unrealistic expectations about somebody\nC) 未提及\nThey may not be prepared for a lifelong relationship. 谈“对终身关系没准备好”，男士讲\n的是“期望不切实际、想改变对方”，从未提“对长期关系的准备”，是另起话头、原文未提。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say about himself as a singer on the road most of his life?", "options": ["A) He is lucky to be able to do what he loves.", "B) He is able to meet many interesting people.", "C) He is able to forget all the troubles in his life.", "D) He is lucky to have visited many exotic places."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） He is lucky to be able to do what he loves.\n【定位】I don't especially love life on the road, but I figure if you are lucky enough to be able to do\nwhat you truly love doing, you've got the ultimate in life.\n我并不特别喜欢这种四处奔波的生活，但我觉得，如果你足够幸运能做自己真正热爱的事，\n你就拥有了人生中最美好的东西。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是真意。男士先说 I don't especially love life on the road，紧跟一个\nbut 转折，转折后的 if you are lucky enough to be able to do what you truly love\ndoing 才是他真正想说的重心。听到 but，落点就在 but 后面。\n【替换】lucky enough to be able to do what you truly love doing ↔ lucky to be able to do what he loves\n几乎原句保留，仅人称转换\n【排除】B) 未提及\nHe is able to meet many interesting people. 说他能遇见许多有趣的人，可男士谈的是\n“能做所爱”，全程没提“遇见有趣的人”，是凭“名人到处跑应该认识很多人”的常识脑补、原文\n未提。\nC) 与原文相反\nHe is able to forget all the troubles in his life. 说他能忘掉生活中所有烦恼，但后文男士\n明说 we have the same troubles they do（我们和普通人一样有烦恼），C 与原文意思相\n反，属正话反说。\n借⽤原⽂：Actually we have the same troubles they do.\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe is lucky to have visited many exotic places. 抄了原文的 lucky，把幸运点偷换成“去\n过很多异国地方”；但男士说的幸运是 lucky enough to be able to do what you truly\nlove doing（能做所爱），与“异域”无关。听到 lucky 就选 D，正中圈套。\n借⽤原⽂：lucky enough to be able to do what you truly love doing", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do most people think of the life of a famous musician?", "options": ["A) It is stressful.", "B) It is all glamour.", "C) It is full of fun.", "D) It is challenging."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） It is all glamour.\n【定位】People think it's all glamour. Actually we have the same troubles they do.\n人们以为这一切都是光鲜亮丽的。可实际上，我们和他们有着一样的烦恼。\n【信号】People think 直接标明这是谁的看法。男士开口就说 People think it's all glamour.，\nPeople think 三个字明确告诉你“这是大众的看法”，正对题干问的 most people；其后\nActually 一转才是他本人的反驳。题目问大众，落点就在 People think 这句。\n【替换】it's all glamour ↔ It is all glamour.\n原句原义，几乎一字不差\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nIt is stressful.“有压力”接近男士本人揭示的真相（we have the same troubles），但题干\n问的是 most people 怎么想，大众想的是光鲜、不是压力。A 把“真相”错当成“大众看法”，\n答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Actually we have the same troubles they do.\nC) 未提及\nIt is full of fun.“充满乐趣”，录音谈的是“光鲜”和“同样有烦恼”，从没说大众觉得它“好玩有\n趣”，属凭印象添加、原文未提。\nD) 未提及\nIt is challenging.“有挑战”，录音里既没说大众这么想、男士也没用过“挑战”描述，纯属凭\n“名人生活想必不易”的常识脑补、原文未提。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How does the man feel whenever he is recognized by his fans?", "options": ["A) Amazed.", "B) Bothered.", "C) Puzzled.", "D) Excited."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） Amazed.\n【定位】But I still find it fascinating. ... I'm still amazed by the love people express for me and\nmy music.\n但我至今仍觉得这件事很奇妙。……我至今仍为人们对我和我的音乐所表达的喜爱而感到\n惊叹。\n【信号】他亲口用的情绪词 amazed。男士说 But I still find it fascinating.，结尾又直说 I'm still\namazed by the love people express for me and my music.，amazed 是他亲口给出的\n情绪词，态度题就抓这个本人用词。\n【替换】I'm still amazed by the love people express ↔ Amazed.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nBothered.“被困扰”是负面情绪，可男士说他 fascinating、amazed，明显是欣喜而非厌\n烦，B 与他的真实感受方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm still amazed by the love people express for me and my music.\nC) 与原文相反\nPuzzled.“困惑”是负面/中性的迷惑情绪，男士表达的是 amazed（惊叹、欣赏），并非“想\n不通”，方向不符。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm still amazed by the love people express for me and my music.\nD) 答非所问\nExcited.“兴奋”也偏正面，和他的好心情同向，最容易和 A 混；但男士原话用的是\namazed/fascinating（惊叹、觉得奇妙），并没说 excited（兴奋），态度题要按他亲口用\n的词来，不能用一个意思接近的词替换。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What should team members do to fully realize their potential?", "options": ["A) Learn to be respectful in a hierarchical organization.", "B) Follow closely the fast development of technology.", "C) Learn new ways of relating and working together.", "D) Maintain the traditional organizational culture."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Learn new ways of relating and working together.\n【定位】Team management calls for new skills if personnel potential is to be fully realized. Although\na team may be composed of knowledgeable people, they must learn new ways of relating and\nworking together to solve cross-functional problems.\n若要充分实现人员潜能，团队管理需要新的技能。尽管团队可能由有知识的人组成，他们也\n必须学会新的相处与协作方式，以解决跨职能的问题。\n【信号】题干关键词 potential 与 must 后接的动作。题干里的 fully realize their potential 在原文\nif personnel potential is to be fully realized 处复现，紧接着的 they must learn new\nways of relating and working together 用 must 抬出\"必须做的动作\"，这个\"must+动\n作\"就是答案信号。\n【替换】learn new ways of relating and working together ↔\nLearn new ways of relating and working together.\n近乎原句的概括复述\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 experienced employees from hierarchical organizations，但那是说\"等级制组\n织出来的人合作未必自然发生\"，并没让成员去\"learn to be respectful（学会恭敬）\"；选\n项盗了 hierarchical 这个声音熟的词，做的却不是录音让成员做的事。\n借⽤原⽂：experienced employees from hierarchical organizations\nB) 偷换概念\n录音开头说 Changing technology and markets have stimulated the team approach，\ntechnology 只是\"催生团队管理\"的大背景，录音从没让团队成员去\"紧跟技术发展\"；选项\n偷换了概念。\n借⽤原⽂：Changing technology and markets have stimulated the team approach to management.\nD) 与原文相反\n原文说守着 traditional organizational culture 的人\"cooperation may not occur\nnaturally（合作未必自然发生）\"，是把\"守传统\"当障碍；选项却让成员\"Maintain the\ntraditional organizational culture（维持传统文化）\"，方向正好反了。\n借⽤原⽂：conditioned to traditional organizational culture, cooperation may not occur naturally", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What needs to be considered for effective team management?", "options": ["A) How the team is built to keep improving its performance.", "B) What type of personnel the team should be composed of.", "C) How the team integrates with what it is supposed to serve.", "D) What qualifications team members should be equipped with."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） How the team integrates with what it is supposed to serve.\n【定位】Furthermore, the issue is not just how the team can function more effectively, but how it\nintegrates with the overall organization, or society that it supposes it serves.\n此外，问题不只是团队如何能更有效地运作，而是它如何与整个组织、或它声称所服务的社\n会相融合。\n【信号】not just…but… 把真意压在 but 之后。原文 the issue is not just how the team can\nfunction more effectively, but how it integrates with… 用 not just…but… 句式，把\"运\n作更有效\"放在 not just 一侧作铺垫，真正强调的\"如何融合\"放在 but 之后，but 后才是答\n案落点。\n【替换】how it integrates with the overall organization, or society that it supposes it serves ↔\nHow the team integrates with what it is supposed to serve.\n把\"组织/社会\"概括为\"所服务对象\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 improve the group's performance 和\"function more effectively\"，但\"运作更\n有效\"被 not just 压成铺垫、不是强调点；选项 keep improving its performance 盗了\nperformance 这个原词，落点恰是录音不强调的那一侧。\n借⽤原⽂：not just how the team can function more effectively\nB) 答非所问\n原文 a team may be composed of knowledgeable people 谈的是成员\"有没有知识\"，不\n是\"该由什么类型人员组成\"这条\"要考虑的事\"；选项 What type of personnel the team\nshould be composed of. 借 composed of 制造熟悉感，却不是题目所问的\"要考虑什\n么\"。\n借⽤原⽂：a team may be composed of knowledgeable people\nD) 未提及\n录音只说团队\"可能由 knowledgeable people 组成\"，从没谈\"成员该具备什么资质\n（qualifications）\"这一\"要考虑的事\"；选项 What qualifications team members should\nbe equipped with. 把 knowledgeable 这点偷换放大成\"资质条件\"，原文无此意。\n借⽤原⽂：a team may be composed of knowledgeable people", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What conclusion can we draw from what Casey says?", "options": ["A) A team manager must set very clear and high objectives.", "B) Teams must consist of members from different cultures.", "C) Team members should be knowledgeable and creative.", "D) A team manager should develop a certain set of skills."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题） A team manager should develop a certain set of skills.\n【定位】They should also cultivate such skills as toleration of ambiguity, persistence and patience, as\nwell as assertiveness. If a team manager exemplifies such qualities, then the team as a whole\nwould be better able to realize their potential and achieve their objectives.\n他们还应培养诸如容忍不确定性、坚持与耐心以及果断这样的技能。如果团队经理体现出这\n些品质，那么整个团队就能更好地实现潜能、达成目标。\n【信号】结尾 if…then… 收束 + 列出一组技能。原文先列 cultivate such skills as toleration of\nambiguity, persistence and patience, as well as assertiveness（一组技能），再用 If a\nteam manager exemplifies such qualities, then the team…would be better able to…\n这个 if…then 句收口；结尾的条件—结果句就是\"得出结论\"的落点。\n【替换】cultivate such skills as toleration of ambiguity, persistence and patience, as well as assertiveness ↔\nA team manager should develop a certain set of skills.\n把具体技能清单概括成\"一套技能\"\nIf a team manager exemplifies such qualities ↔ A team manager\n结论主体同为 team manager\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文结尾只说经理体现品质后团队能 achieve their objectives（达成目标），从没说经理\n要\"set very clear and high objectives（设定清晰而高的目标）\"；选项 must set very\nclear and high objectives 盗了 objectives 这个原词，把\"达成目标\"偷换成\"设定高目标\"。\n借⽤原⽂：achieve their objectives\nB) 过度推断\n原文说经理 multi-cultural managers exhibit understanding of their own and others'\ncultural influences，谈的是经理要理解文化差异，并没要求\"团队必须由不同文化的成员组\n成\"；选项 Teams must consist of members from different cultures. 借 cultural 这个\n词，又用 must consist of 绝对化，原文无此结论。\n借⽤原⽂：multi-cultural managers exhibit understanding of their own and others' cultural\ninfluences and limitations\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 a team may be composed of knowledgeable people 的\"有知识\"说的是普通成员的\n现有状态，而 Casey 这段结论强调的主体是 team manager 该练技能；选项 Team\nmembers should be knowledgeable and creative. 把焦点错安到\"成员\"身上，且\ncreative 原文未提。\n借⽤原⽂：a team may be composed of knowledgeable people", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is probably the best form of advertising according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Word-of-mouth advertising.", "B) Distributing free trial products.", "C) Prestige advertising.", "D) Institutional advertising."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Word-of-mouth advertising.\n【定位】The best form of advertising is probably word-of-mouth advertising which occurs when\npeople tell their friends about the benefits of products or services that they have purchased.\n最好的广告形式很可能是口碑广告，它发生在人们把自己买过的产品或服务的好处告诉朋友\n的时候。\n【信号】最高级 the best 直接标出答案。The best form of advertising is probably word-of-\nmouth advertising 中 the best 这个最高级就是答案信号——录音用最高级把某一项单独\n拎出来评价，最高级后面紧跟的对象（word-of-mouth advertising）就是答案。\n【替换】The best form of advertising is probably word-of-mouth advertising ↔\nWord-of-mouth advertising.\n原词整体照搬为选项\n【排除】B) 未提及\nDistributing free trial products.（派发免费试用品）在整段录音里从未出现，属凭常识臆\n造的干扰项，与最好的广告形式无关。\nC) 原词陷阱\nPrestige advertising. 确实是录音念到的原词（institutional or prestige advertising），\n但录音说它是用来建立声望、并不是说它最好，盗用原词来骗听到就选。\n借⽤原⽂：institutional or prestige advertising\nD) 原词陷阱\nInstitutional advertising. 也是录音原词（institutional or prestige advertising），录音\n只是把它和 prestige 并列、说其作用是建声望，从未说它是最好的，同属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：institutional or prestige advertising", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say is the purpose of many organizations using prestige advertising?", "options": ["A) To sell a particular product.", "B) To attract high-end consumers.", "C) To promote a specific service.", "D) To build up their reputation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） To build up their reputation.\n【定位】Indeed, many organizations also use institutional or prestige advertising which is designed to\nbuild up their reputation rather than to sell particular products.\n事实上，许多机构还会使用机构广告或声望广告，其目的是建立自己的声誉，而不是销售具\n体产品。\n【信号】designed to 加 rather than 点目的。which is designed to build up their reputation\nrather than to sell particular products 中 designed to 直接给出目的，rather than 又把\n真目的（建声望）与假目的（卖货）摆在对立两端，肯定的一端就是答案。\n【替换】designed to build up their reputation ↔ To build up their reputation.\ndesigned to 改写为 To do\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nTo sell a particular product. 对应录音 rather than to sell particular products，但这恰\n恰是 rather than 后面被否定的那一端，录音说声望广告的目的不是卖货，选它就反了。\n借⽤原⽂：rather than to sell particular products\nB) 过度推断\nTo attract high-end consumers.（吸引高端客户）在录音中从未出现，是把 prestige（声\n望）一词联想成高档客户的过度推断。\nC) 偷换概念\nTo promote a specific service.（推广某项具体服务）借用了录音里 products or services\n的 service 字眼，但录音说声望广告的目的不是推销具体产品或服务，方向不符。\n借⽤原⽂：to sell particular products", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "How do large companies generally handle their advertising?", "options": ["A) By creating their own ads and commercials.", "B) By buying media space in leading newspapers.", "C) By hiring their own professional advertising staff.", "D) By using the services of large advertising agencies."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By using the services of large advertising agencies.\n【定位】Although large companies could easily set up their own advertising departments, write their\nown advertisements and buy media space themselves, they tend to use the services of large\nadvertising agencies.\n尽管大公司完全可以轻松地建立自己的广告部门、自己撰写广告并自己购买媒体版面，但它\n们往往会使用大型广告代理公司的服务。\n【信号】Although 让步后 tend to 才是常规。Although large companies could easily set up\ntheir own advertising departments…, they tend to use the services of large\nadvertising agencies 中 Although 引出让步（自办是能做但不是常规），转折后的 tend\nto（往往）才是大公司真正惯常的做法。\n【替换】they tend to use the services of large advertising agencies ↔\nBy using the services of large advertising agencies.\ntend to 改写为 By doing\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBy creating their own ads and commercials. 对应录音 write their own\nadvertisements，但这是 Although 让步从句里描述大公司能做却不常做的事，不是真正\n的常规做法。\n借⽤原⽂：write their own advertisements\nB) 原词陷阱\nBy buying media space in leading newspapers. 借用录音 buy media space\nthemselves 的原词，但同样属于让步从句里大公司自办的内容，且 in leading\nnewspapers 是录音没说的添加。\n借⽤原⽂：buy media space themselves\nC) 与原文相反\nBy hiring their own professional advertising staff.（自雇广告人员）呼应录音 set up\ntheir own advertising departments 的自办思路，但这正是录音用 tend to 否定掉的方\n向，与最终常规做法相反。\n借⽤原⽂：set up their own advertising departments", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What will advertising agencies often do before a national campaign?", "options": ["A) Conduct a large-scale survey on customer needs.", "B) Specify the objectives of the campaign in detail.", "C) Pre-test alternative ads or commercials in certain regions.", "D) Decide on what specific means of communication to employ."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Pre-test alternative ads or commercials in certain regions.\n【定位】Agencies often produce alternative ads or commercials that are pre-tested in newspapers,\ntelevision stations etc., in different parts of the country before a final choice is made prior to a\nnational campaign.\n代理公司常常制作多个备选广告，先在全国不同地区的报纸、电视台等媒体上进行预先测\n试，然后才做出最终选择，再进行全国性的投放。\n【信号】prior to a national campaign 对上题干 before。pre-tested … in different parts of\nthe country before a final choice is made prior to a national campaign 中 prior to a\nnational campaign 与题干 before a national campaign 直接对应，全国投放之前发生的\n动作就是 pre-test。\n【替换】alternative ads or commercials that are pre-tested … in different parts of the country ↔\nPre-test alternative ads or commercials in certain regions.\nin different parts of the country 概括为 in certain regions\n【排除】A) 未提及\nConduct a large-scale survey on customer needs.（做大规模客户需求调查）录音从未\n提及，是凭广告业常识臆造的内容。\nB) 张冠李戴\nSpecify the objectives of the campaign in detail. 盗用录音 a statement of the\nobjectives of the advertising campaign 的 objectives 原词，但定目标是客户公司给代理\n公司的 brief，不是代理公司在全国投放前做的，做的人和时间都不对。\n借⽤原⽂：a statement of the objectives of the advertising campaign\nD) 原词陷阱\nDecide on what specific means of communication to employ. 呼应录音 develops a\nmedia plan, specifying which media will be used，定媒介确实是代理公司做的，但那是\n制定媒体计划阶段的事，不是题干问的全国投放前的预测试动作，时间点不符。\n借⽤原⽂：develops a media plan, specifying which media will be used", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "According to Dr. Wood, what is essential to forming a good family atmosphere?", "options": ["A) The effective communication among family members.", "B) The principle of \"avoid adding trouble to others\".", "C) The mutual love and caring between parents and children.", "D) Everyone's commitment to support each other."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The effective communication among family members.\n【定位】Highly functional successful family groups tend to be those that communicate effectively,\nwhile the lack of such communication is frequently found in troubled families, according to a\nresearch company.\n高效成功的家庭往往是那些能有效沟通的家庭，而缺乏这种沟通则常见于问题家庭，据一家\n研究机构所言。\n【信号】对比句点出关键变量。这句用 while 把\"成功家庭\"与\"问题家庭\"对照，两边唯一差的变量就\n是 communicate effectively——这种\"有它则成功、缺它则出问题\"的对比，正是把某要素\n定为\"关键\"的信号。\n【替换】successful family groups tend to be those that communicate effectively ↔\nThe effective communication among family members\n动词短语改写成名词短语\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThe principle of \"avoid adding trouble to others\" 这条原则原文从未出现，属凭常识臆\n造的善良准则，听上去顺耳但无依据。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe mutual love and caring between parents and children 里 parents and children 很\n像原文家庭话题，但原文定为关键的是\"有效沟通\"不是\"父母子女间的爱与关怀\"，它换了对\n象、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：within a household\nD) 未提及\nEveryone's commitment to support each other 把家庭关系归因于\"彼此支持的承诺\"，\n原文谈的关键是沟通，从未提到\"承诺支持\"，属另造一个合理但无据的要素。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Which of the following is helpful for minimizing outside distractions during communication?", "options": ["A) Thinking before speaking.", "B) Adopting empathy during communication.", "C) Focusing on the present.", "D) Listening attentively."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Focusing on the present.\n【定位】A sharp focus on the topic at hand is essential for clear and effective communication, and it is\nalso helpful to keep outside distractions to a minimum.\n对眼前话题的高度专注，对清晰有效的沟通至关重要，它也有助于把外界干扰降到最低。\n【信号】题干原词在原文出现并被绑定做法。原文这句几乎照搬题干的 keep outside distractions\nto a minimum，并把它和 A sharp focus on the topic at hand（专注眼前话题）绑在一\n起——题干的效果词在原文出现、且紧挨着给出做法，这就是答案句信号。\n【替换】A sharp focus on the topic at hand ↔ Focusing on the present\n同义改写：当下的话题=present\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThinking before speaking 对应的是 Think First, Speak Later 那条建议，讲的是\"说前先\n组织思路\"，与本题问的\"减少外界干扰\"不是一回事，被挪到了别的分论点上。\n借⽤原⽂：Think First, Speak Later\nB) 原词陷阱\nAdopting empathy during communication 里 empathy 确在原文出现，但是在\nempathy for the other person 那段讲\"先想后说\"时提到的，与减少外界干扰无关，是听到\n原词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：empathy for the other person or persons involved\nD) 答非所问\nListening attentively 对应 Listen Actively 那条\"积极倾听\"建议，是另一个分论点，跟\"减\n少外界干扰\"不搭，属把别条建议错配到本题。\n借⽤原⽂：Listen Actively", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "Which of the following should be avoided in order to make a conversation smooth?", "options": ["A) Adopting nonverbal signals.", "B) Being aggressive or overly stressed.", "C) Maintaining eye contact to show attentiveness.", "D) Keeping a suitable distance and respecting personal space."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Being aggressive or overly stressed.\n【定位】One way to ensure that nonverbal signals don't undermine verbal communication is to remain\ncalm and avoid becoming overly stressed during a discussion.\n确保非语言信号不破坏言语沟通的一个办法，是在讨论中保持冷静、避免变得过度紧张。\n【信号】avoid 直接点名要躲开的行为。这句里 avoid becoming overly stressed 用 avoid 明确点\n出\"要避开\"的对象，正对题干的 should be avoided；前文 an unintentionally aggressive\nnonverbal cue 也把 aggressive 列为坏信号，两处都指向\"咄咄逼人/过度紧张\"。\n【替换】an unintentionally aggressive nonverbal cue ↔ Being aggressive or overly stressed\n两个原词原样合进选项\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nAdopting nonverbal signals 盗用本段 Use Nonverbal Cues 的话题，但原文是叫人\"用\n好\"非语言信号，不是叫人避免；本题问该避免什么，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：Use Nonverbal Cues\nC) 与原文相反\nMaintaining eye contact to show attentiveness 直接挪用原文 maintaining a\ncomfortable amount of eye contact，但原文把它当\"该做\"的好习惯，本题问该避免什\n么，选它就是把好做法误当坏事。\n借⽤原⽂：maintaining a comfortable amount of eye contact\nD) 与原文相反\nKeeping a suitable distance and respecting personal space 对应原文 respecting\npersonal space，同样是原文提倡的好做法，与\"该避免\"相反。\n借⽤原⽂：respecting personal space", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What is essential to be a good listener?", "options": ["A) To make sure you turn off your cell phones.", "B) To respect people involved in the conversation.", "C) To show you are an easy-going person.", "D) To offer suggestions actively."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To respect people involved in the conversation.\n【定位】In addition to avoiding obvious distractions such as cell phones and using body language to\ndemonstrate attentiveness, it is also important to allow the other person to finish speaking\nbefore formulating our own response.\n除了避免手机这类明显的干扰、用肢体语言表现专注之外，更重要的是让对方把话说完，再\n组织我们自己的回应。\n【信号】important to 标出关键倾听行为。原文用 it is also important to allow the other\nperson to finish speaking，把\"让对方把话讲完\"标为重要的倾听行为；前面 cell phones\n等只是顺带提的\"次要事\"，真正强调的关键在 important to 之后。\n【替换】allow the other person to finish speaking before formulating our own response ↔\nTo respect people involved in the conversation\n具体行为升华为其本质\"尊重\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo make sure you turn off your cell phones 里 cell phones 确在原文出现，但原文只把\n它列为要 avoiding obvious distractions such as cell phones 的次要事，且说的是\"避免\n干扰\"不是\"关掉手机\"，并非本题问的\"关键\"，是听到手机就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：avoiding obvious distractions such as cell phones\nC) 未提及\nTo show you are an easy-going person 把倾听目的说成\"显得自己随和\"，原文谈倾听是\n为了尊重对方、听懂对方，从没提\"显得随和\"，属凭印象臆造的目的。\nD) 与原文相反\nTo offer suggestions actively（主动提建议）恰与原文 refraining from offering advice\nunless asked to do so（除非被请教，否则别给建议）相反，把原文叫你\"别做\"的事说成了\n该做，极性反了。\n借⽤原⽂：refraining from offering advice unless asked to do so", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Among human's various abilities, which one has the same mechanism as mental health?", "options": ["A) The ability of speaking a certain kind of language.", "B) The ability of mending things.", "C) The ability of building a good relationship.", "D) The ability of healing physical wounds."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The ability of healing physical wounds.\n【定位】We don't have to learn how to be mentally healthy; it is built into us in the same way that our\nbodies know how to heal a cut or mend a broken bone.\n我们不必学习如何保持心理健康；它天生就内建于我们体内，就像我们的身体本能地知道如\n何愈合伤口、接合断骨一样。\n【信号】明喻信号 in the same way that。答案句靠明喻信号 in the same way that（\"就像……一\n样\"）锁定——它后面紧跟的 our bodies know how to heal a cut or mend a broken\nbone 就是与心理健康机制相同的那种能力，正是题干要的对应。\n【替换】our bodies know how to heal a cut or mend a broken bone ↔ healing physical wounds\n愈合伤口=身体疗愈\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 speaking a certain kind of language 看似呼应原文，其实原文末段 Mental health\nhas commonly been called conscience, instinct, wisdom, common sense, or the\ninner voice. 谈的是它被叫作 \"内在声音\"，并未说它像 \"说某种语言的能力\"，属答非所问的\n诱选。\n借⽤原⽂：the inner voice\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文 mend a broken bone 里的 mend 是 \"接合断骨\"（仍属身体自愈），B 却把它偷换成\nmending things \"修理东西\"，张冠李戴，机制并不相同。\n借⽤原⽂：mend a broken bone\nC) 未提及\nC 的 building a good relationship 虽与第2段 \"sympathy、kindness\" 等氛围沾边，但原\n文从未说 mental health 的 \"机制\" 与 \"建立良好关系的能力\" 相同，是无中生有的拔高。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "Among the following mental statuses, which can be the indication of possessing mental health?", "options": ["A) Always feeling miserable when encountering difficulties.", "B) Keeping calm though the child has made the house a mess.", "C) Unwilling to offer a hand to those in troubles.", "D) Feeling superior to everybody else."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Keeping calm though the child has made the house a mess.\n【定位】It gives us patience for ourselves and toward others as well as patience while driving,\ncatching a fish, working on our car, or raising a child.\n它（心理健康）赋予我们对自己、对他人的耐心，也让我们在开车、钓鱼、修车或养育孩子\n时都保有耐心。\n【信号】列举信号 patience while ...raising a child。答案句靠 \"心理健康赋予什么\" 的列举落点定\n位：patience while driving, catching a fish, working on our car, or raising a child 把\n\"养孩子时仍有耐心\" 列为心理健康的表现，正对应 B 的 \"孩子把家弄乱仍冷静\"。\n【替换】patience while ... raising a child ↔ Keeping calm though the child has made the house a mess\n养孩子有耐心=乱中保持冷静\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文谈 view others with sympathy if they are having troubles 是说 \"别人有难时报以同\n情\"，A 却说 \"自己遇难总是痛苦\"——盗用 troubles 一词却把方向反成消极心态，与心理健\n康相悖。\n借⽤原⽂：if they are having troubles\nC) 与原文相反\n原文是 view others with sympathy if they are having troubles, with kindness if they\nare in pain，即愿意关怀帮助他人；C 的 Unwilling to offer a hand to those in troubles\n\"不愿帮处于困境的人\" 恰好相反，是把原意反着说。\n借⽤原⽂：with kindness if they are in pain\nD) 与原文相反\n原文说心理健康让人 not take ourselves too seriously, to laugh at ourselves（不把自己\n太当回事、能自嘲），D 的 Feeling superior to everybody else \"觉得高人一等\" 与这种谦\n和态度正相反，原文也根本没说傲慢是其标志。\n借⽤原⽂：not take ourselves too seriously", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "Which of the following is the best title for this passage?", "options": ["A) Mental Health: Tool Case of the Mind", "B) How to Gain Mental Health", "C) Mental Health: The Key to Success", "D) Be Careful of Mental Problems"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） Mental Health: Tool Case of the Mind\n【定位】Mental health is the seed that contains self-esteem—confidence in ourselves and an ability\nto trust in our common sense. Mental health is the source of creativity for solving problems,\nresolving conflict... It gives us patience... It allows us to see the beauty that surrounds us\neach moment.\n心理健康是蕴含自尊的种子——是对自己的信心、对常识的信任；它是解决问题、化解冲突\n的创造力之源……它赋予我们耐心……它让我们看见每一刻环绕我们的美。\n【信号】全段排比 Mental health is / It gives / It allows。全文（尤其第2段）用 Mental health\nis the source of creativity、It gives us patience、It allows us to see the beauty 等排\n比，反复罗列心理健康 \"能装下/能产出\" 的种种好处——这种 \"一物之内盛着诸多功用\" 的\n结构，正是 \"工具箱\" 标题的依据。\n【替换】Mental health is the source of creativity for solving problems, resolving conflict ↔\nMental Health: Tool Case of the Mind\n诸多功用之源=心智工具箱\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 的 How to Gain Mental Health 盗用了第1段 we don't know how to gain access to it\n里的 gain，但全文重心是讲心理健康 \"是什么、有什么用\"，几乎没教 \"如何获得\" 的方法，\nB 以局部当全篇。\n借⽤原⽂：we don't know how to gain access to it\nC) 未提及\nC 的 Mental Health: The Key to Success 把心理健康说成 \"成功之钥\"，但全文谈的是耐\n心、看得开、内在平和、快乐生活，从未把它与 \"success（成功）\" 挂钩，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 的 Be Careful of Mental Problems 谈 \"警惕心理问题\"，是负面警示口吻；而全文自始\n至终在正面赞美心理健康的种种好处（Mental health is our birthright），基调相反，标\n题跑偏。\n借⽤原⽂：Mental health is our birthright", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is the fundamental step of career planning?", "options": ["A) Studying hard for a relatively high degree.", "B) Having enough knowledge about different careers.", "C) Understanding the new trend of career planning.", "D) Knowing more about yourself."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Knowing more about yourself.\n【定位】Study yourself. This is the key to career planning. Understanding what you are like, what you\nvalue, and what kind of person you want to become is the foundation for all career planning.\n研究你自己。这是职业规划的关键。理解你是怎样的人、你看重什么、你想成为什么样的\n人，是一切职业规划的基础。\n【信号】key+foundation 双重'最根本'信号。这句连用了两个排序信号：This is the key to career\nplanning 的 key（关键）和 is the foundation for all career planning 的 foundation\n（基础），都在说'Study yourself 是最根本的一步'，正好对应题干的 fundamental step，\n是典型的'信号词锁答案句'。\n【替换】Study yourself ↔ Knowing more about yourself\nstudy yourself=know yourself 同义改写\nUnderstanding what you are like, what you value, and what kind of person you want to become ↔\nKnowing more about yourself\n把一长串自我认识概括成 know yourself\n【排除】A) 未提及\nStudying hard for a relatively high degree 把'努力考高学历'当成最根本一步，这是考生\n最容易凭常识填的答案，但录音从头到尾在讲'研究自己、定目标、排时间表'，根本没说要\n先考高学历，属于用常识代替原文的典型坑。\nB) 原词陷阱\nHaving enough knowledge about different careers 借了录音反复出现的 career 一词制\n造耳熟。原文确实说要 learn both about ourselves and about work，但强调的是先'了解\n自己'这一根本，'了解工作/职业'是与之并列的另一半，不是被抬到 key/foundation 位置的\n那一项，故答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：learn both about ourselves and about work\nC) 原词陷阱\nUnderstanding the new trend of career planning 借了 career planning 这个贯穿全文\n的词组充耳熟，但录音只讲怎么做规划的步骤，从未提'新趋势 new trend'，是把原词拼上\n一个原文没有的概念。\n借⽤原⽂：how to do career planning", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "According to the speaker, what should you do if your ultimate aim is rather ambitious?", "options": ["A) Ask for professional advice from experienced people.", "B) Bring it down to a practical level.", "C) Divide it into smaller and achievable goals.", "D) Find an objective standard to reevaluate your ability."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Divide it into smaller and achievable goals.\n【定位】Many will probably lead to a series of goals that you must accomplish over time to reach your\ndesired end result. If your ultimate goal is to have a managerial role, you may need to become\na trainer first, then a supervisor.\n很多目标很可能会引出一连串你必须逐步完成的目标，才能达到你想要的最终结果。如果你\n的终极目标是当上管理者，你可能需要先成为一名培训员，再成为一名主管。\n【信号】举例信号：用 managerial role 拆成 trainer→supervisor。原文用 If your ultimate\ngoal is to have a managerial role 直接呼应题干的 ultimate aim，并用一个例子演示把大\n目标拆成小步骤——a series of goals that you must accomplish over time 和 become\na trainer first, then a supervisor，这个'大目标→一连串小目标'的举例就是答案信号。\n【替换】a series of goals that you must accomplish over time ↔\nDivide it into smaller and achievable goals\n'一连串需逐步完成的目标'=拆成小而可实现的目标\nbecome a trainer first, then a supervisor ↔ smaller and achievable goals\ntrainer→supervisor 的台阶=smaller goals\n【排除】A) 未提及\nAsk for professional advice from experienced people 是生活里应对难题最顺的反应，\n但录音针对'大目标'给的是'自己拆成小目标',从未让你去请教有经验的人，属于用常识补原文\n的坑。\nB) 偷换概念\nBring it down to a practical level（让它落到可操作层面）和正确项意思相近、最易混，\n但原文针对大目标讲的具体动作是'分解成一连串小目标'（divide），不是泛泛的'落实到可\n操作'；B 把具体的'拆分'换成了模糊的'落实'，方向虽近却不是原文那个动作。\n借⽤原⽂：lead to a series of goals that you must accomplish over time\nD) 未提及\nFind an objective standard to reevaluate your ability 听着像在谈能力评估，但录音针对\n大目标讲的是'分解目标'，从未提到要'找客观标准重新评估你的能力 reevaluate your\nability'，是凭'宏大目标→评估能力'的联想编出来的。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is the meaning of making career plans?", "options": ["A) To help you to deal with new problems you may meet with in your career better.", "B) To ensure that you achieve something big sometime in the future.", "C) To shorten the time you are tangled up with tricky situations.", "D) To accelerate the process of climbing up the social ladder."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nTo help you to deal with new problems you may meet with in your career better.\n【定位】Career planning does not guarantee that all the problems, difficulties, or decision-making\nsituations that you face in the future will be solved. But career planning should help you to\napproach and cope better with new problems, such as deciding whether or not to enter\neducational or training programs, deciding whether or not to change jobs.\n职业规划并不能保证你将来面对的所有问题、困难或需要决策的情形都会被解决。但职业规\n划应该能帮助你更好地应对处理你遇到的新问题，比如决定是否进入教育或培训项目、决定\n是否换工作。\n【信号】转折 But 之后才是真意。原文先用 does not guarantee that all the problems… will be\nsolved 否定一种夸大说法，紧接 But career planning should help you to approach and\ncope better with new problems 用 But 转折，转折后的这句才是讲者对'规划意义'的真正\n落点——典型的'转折后才是答案'信号。\n【替换】help you to approach and cope better with new problems ↔\nhelp you to deal with new problems you may meet with in your career better\ncope better with=deal with…better 同义替换\nshould help you ↔ To help you\nshould help=help，留余地的语气一致\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nTo ensure that you achieve something big 的 ensure 与原文 does not guarantee 的\nguarantee 撞车，但原文是'并不保证'，B 却说成'保证你成就大事'，极性正好反了——原文\n否定的夸大说法被它当成了答案。\n借⽤原⽂：does not guarantee that all the problems… will be solved\nC) 原词陷阱\nTo shorten the time you are tangled up with tricky situations 借了原文反复出现的\nproblems、situations 充耳熟，但原文只说帮你'更好地应对'新问题，从没说能'缩短你陷在\n棘手处境里的时间 shorten the time'，shorten 这个量化承诺是凭空加的。\n借⽤原⽂：cope better with new problems, such as… analyzing the difficulties you are having with\na situation or a person\nD) 过度推断\nTo accelerate the process of climbing up the social ladder 听着励志，但录音讲的意义\n是'更好应对新问题'，并未承诺职业规划能'加速向上爬社会阶梯 climbing up the social\nladder'，是把规划的作用夸张引申。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) exception\n名词\n【词性槽】be no __ 结构后接名词\n【依据】be no exception 是地道固定搭配\n上文先说 It seems to be a law in the technology industry that leading companies\neventually lose their positions——技术行业领头公司终会失势是\"通则\"；后半句举诺基亚\n为该\"通则\"的例证，意为\"诺基亚也不例外\"，正好对应 be no exception 这一高频短语。\n【竞争词】B) bias 义偏\"偏见\"，与通则话题不搭\nbe no bias 无此固定搭配，且 bias 指主观偏向，与\"诺基亚也不例外\"这一逻辑无关。\nD) deterioration 留给 35 题描述文化\"恶化\"\ndeterioration 指过程性的衰退，第 35 题 This contributed to the __(35)__ of the company's\nonce-spirited culture 才是它的语义归宿，此处放在 be no 后不通。\nF) fault 留给 31 题 at fault 短语\nfault 在本篇要去搭 31 题的 But Elop was not the only person at __(31)__.——固定短语 at\nfault，此处填进来既丢搭配又丢逻辑。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be no __ 结构后接名词"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) worldwide\n副词\n【词性槽】句末修饰整句的状语\n【依据】mobile phone sales worldwide = 全球手机销量\n上下文讲诺基亚 2007 年占全球手机销量四成多，与下文 With the introduction of\nApple's iPhone in the middle of that year, Nokia's market share __(29)__ rapidly 同台\n对比的也是全球市场。worldwide 作副词后置修饰 sales，地道且表全球范围。\n【竞争词】G) incidentally \"顺便提一句\"与正文不搭\nincidentally 多用于句首插入语，表示话题切换的口语化副词，与这种正式数据陈述句风格\n不符。\nH) notably \"尤其、特别是\"通常前置举例\nnotably 常用于举例引出对象（如 ..., notably Jorma Ollila），留给 32 题；置于句末修饰 sales\n既不自然也丢搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句末修饰整句的状语"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) shifting\n动词\n【词性槽】were already __ 后接进行时分词\n【依据】preferences shift toward = 偏好转向\nshift toward 是地道搭配，表\"转向、偏向\"。本句 consumers' preferences were already\n__(28)__ toward touch-screen smartphones. 紧接下文 With the introduction of\nApple's iPhone... Nokia's market share __(29)__ rapidly 描述 iPhone 上市后份额暴跌\n——偏好早已在向智能机倾斜，正是 shifting 之义。\n【竞争词】N) transmitting \"传输\"对象通常是信号/信息\ntransmit 的宾语是 signal/disease/information 等，与 preferences（偏好）搭配既不地道，\n又无法表达\"转向\"之义；transmitting toward 也不构成短语。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·were already __ 后接进行"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) shrank\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 market share 后缺谓语动词\n【依据】\"市场份额缩水\"是地道搭配\nshare/market shrank rapidly = \"市场份额迅速萎缩\"。与下半句 revenue plunged（收入\n暴跌）构成同义并列，共同描绘 iPhone 上市后诺基亚的颓势。\n【竞争词】J) relayed \"转达\"是及物动词，需要宾语\nrelay 后须接信息类宾语，本句空后是副词 rapidly，结构上当不及物动词使用，relay 无此用\n法；语义上\"市场份额转达\"也荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 market share 后缺谓语"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) assumed\n动词\n【词性槽】which 引导的定语从句缺谓语\n【依据】assume a position = 就任职位\n先行词是 his position as CEO，assume the position 是\"就任该职位\"的固定搭配。结合\n...a series of decisions made by Stephen Elop in his position as CEO, which he\n__(30)__ in October 2010.，意为\"他于 2010 年 10 月就任的 CEO 一职\"，逻辑通顺。\n【竞争词】J) relayed \"转达\"与 position 不搭\nrelay 的宾语通常是 message/information/signal，与 position（职位）不构成搭配，语义\n不通。\nL) shrank shrink 是不及物或义为缩小\nshrank 表\"缩小、萎缩\"，且为不及物动词为主，无法直接带 position 作宾语；它要留给 29 题\n描述\"市场份额缩水\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·which 引导的定语从句缺谓语"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) fault\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 at 后接名词\n【依据】at fault = 有过错\n本段语境 But Elop was not the only person at __(31)__. Nokia's board resisted\nchange...——\"但 Elop 不是唯一有过错的人\"，引出董事会同样要担责，这正是固定短语 at\nfault（有过错/该负责）。\n【竞争词】B) bias at bias 无此搭配\nbias 多与 against/toward 连用，与 at 不搭，且义为\"偏见\"，无法表达\"该负责\"。\nD) deterioration 义为衰退，留给 35 题\ndeterioration 不与 at 构成短语，且其核心义\"恶化\"显然对应文化崩塌那一空。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 at 后接名词"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) notably\n副词\n【词性槽】句首独立的修饰副词\n【依据】most notably = \"尤其值得一提\"承接前句\n上句 Nokia's board resisted change, making it impossible for the company to adapt\nto rapid shifts in the industry. 概述董事会整体守旧；接着 Most notably 引出最典型代表\nJorma Ollila——是\"尤其、特别是\"的并列举例语义，紧接其后的人名印证这一逻辑。\n【竞争词】G) incidentally \"顺便提一下\"与举例语义反向\nincidentally 表附带、不重要，但下文 Jorma Ollila 显然是被重点点名批评，恰恰是\"最显著\n的\"，二者方向冲突。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— incidentally 与 notably 句式都对。Most incidentally 在\n句式上看起来也通，但语义相反：incidentally 表\"附带提一句\"，是把对象往低优先级推；\n而本段下文要重点点名 Jorma Ollila 是董事会守旧的典型代表，指向最重要、最显著。防\n坑：副词候选要看下文是\"重点举例\"还是\"附带补充\"，方向一锚定，就只能是 notably。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句首独立的修饰副词"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) previous\n形容词\n【词性槽】the company's __ success 作定语\n【依据】\"以往的成功\"才导致沉迷不思变\n上句 Jorma Ollila, who had led Nokia's transition from an industrial company to a\ntechnology giant 说他曾领导诺基亚转型为科技巨头——这是\"以往的辉煌\"；too\nfascinated... to recognize the change 表他沉迷于此辉煌而忽视变革。previous success\n（以往的成功）精确对应这一过去基业的语义。\n【竞争词】M) subtle \"微妙的成功\"语义怪异\nsubtle success 不是常用搭配，且无法解释 Ollila 为何\"沉迷过头\"——subt le 强调细微，反而会\n让人忽视，不至于沉迷其中。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the company's __ suc"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) desperate\n形容词\n【词性槽】a __ + 名词 中间作定语\n【依据】\"孤注一掷的\"裁员才致文化崩塌\n下文 which included the elimination of thousands of jobs. 描述裁员上千岗位，规模与\n后果都极端；又有 This contributed to the __(35)__ of the company's once-spirited\nculture 说这直接导致文化恶化——只有 desperate（绝望/孤注一掷的）才能与如此剧烈的\n成本削减及其后果匹配。\n【竞争词】I) previous \"先前的\"与具体动作不搭\nprevious cost-cutting program 表\"先前的削减项目\"，但本句首词 also 表明这是新动作，而非\n追溯过去；previous 留给 33 题描述 Ollila 沉迷的\"以往成功\"。\nM) subtle \"微妙的\"程度过弱\nsubtle 强调不易察觉的细微，与一刀切上千岗位的剧烈程度严重不符，方向完全错。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— subtle 同为形容词易被误选。考生若只盯 a __ cost-\ncutting program 的语法形式，subtle、desperate、previous 都\"装得下\"。防坑：看后果\n——included the elimination of thousands of jobs 和 contributed to the __(35)__ 显\n示该措施剧烈且引发严重后果，subtle 程度太弱、previous 时间错位，唯有 desperate 与\n后果剧烈度匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a __ + 名词 中间作定语"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assumed", "B) bias", "C) desperate", "D) deterioration", "E) exception", "F) fault", "G) incidentally", "H) notably", "I) previous", "J) relayed", "K) shifting", "L) shrank", "M) subtle", "N) transmitting", "O) worldwide"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) deterioration\n名词\n【词性槽】the __ of 结构后接名词\n【依据】\"文化的恶化\"承接上文裁员后果\n本段先说激进裁员 The company also embarked on a __(34)__ cost-cutting program,\nwhich included the elimination of thousands of jobs.，接着用 This contributed to 引\n出后果，又说 Good leaders left the company... much of Nokia's most valuable design\nand programming talent left as well.——文化由盛转衰，正是 deterioration（恶化）\n一词。\n【竞争词】B) bias \"偏见\"与文化崩塌无关\nthe bias of the culture（文化的偏见）逻辑不通；本段讲文化由\"曾经富有进取精神\"到人才出\n走，是过程性衰退，不是偏向。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the __ of 结构后接名词"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Many first-generation college-goers have doubts about their abilities to get a college degree.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】对学位能力有 doubts\n【定位】Many students are coming from a situation where no one around them has the experience of\nsuccessfully completing higher education, so they are coming in questioning themselves and\ntheir college worthiness.\n许多学生身处一种环境——身边没有任何人成功读完过高等教育——所以他们入学时就开始\n怀疑自己以及自己是否配得上大学。\n【改写】doubts about their abilities ↔ questioning themselves\ndoubts ↔ questioning\nto get a college degree ↔ college worthiness\n拿学位 ↔ 配得上大学\n【辨邻】 L L 段 Chelsea Jones 说自己 wanted to go to college but I didn't really know the\nprocess——讲的是不了解升学流程，不是怀疑自己读完大学的能力；与题干 doubts\nabout their abilities 是不同侧面，话题相邻但不等值。\nI wanted to go to college but I didn't really know the process\nO O 段写 lacked confidence about \"belonging\" at such a top institution，但主语是\nmentors, staff, and professors 在提供支持去化解学生的不自信；陈述对象是“学校的\n支持”而非“学生自己怀疑”，与题干 Q36 的语义焦点不同。\nlacked confidence about \"belonging\" at such a top institution", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "First-generation college students tend to have much heavier financial burdens than their peers.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】经济负担比同龄人重\n【定位】They typically carry financial burdens that outweigh those of their peers, are more likely to\nwork while attending school, and often require significant academic remediation.\n他们通常承担的经济负担超过同龄人，更可能边读书边打工，且往往需要大量学业补习。\n【改写】much heavier financial burdens ↔ financial burdens that outweigh those of their peers\nheavier ↔ outweigh\nthan their peers ↔ those of their peers\n比较对象同指同龄人\n【辨邻】 A A 段提到 Nijay worked between 30 and 40 hours a week，是个例打工细节、且没\n有把第一代学生与同龄人作经济负担比较；A 段只能覆盖“打工”局部，覆盖不到题干\nQ37 的“群体比较”。\nworked between 30 and 40 hours a week\nB B 段 lost his Pell Grant of over $5,000 是 Nijay 个人遭遇，没有形成 much heavier\n... than their peers 这种群体级比较结构。\nNijay lost his Pell Grant of over $5,000", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The graduation rate of first-generation students at Nijay's university was incredibly low.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Nijay 学校毕业率极低\n【定位】What Nijay didn't realize about his school—Tennessee State University—was its\nfrighteningly low graduation rate a mere 29 percent for its first-generation students.\nNijay 当时没意识到自己学校（田纳西州立大学）有一个吓人地低的毕业率——第一代学生\n只有 29%。\n【改写】Nijay's university ↔ Tennessee State University\n代称落到具体校名\nincredibly low ↔ frighteningly low\nincredibly ↔ frighteningly\ngraduation rate of first-generation students ↔\ngraduation rate a mere 29 percent for its first-generation students\n限定语原样对位\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然也讲第一代学生毕业率低——graduates just 16 percent of its first-\ngeneration students——但主语是 The University of Tennessee in Knoxville，不是\nNijay 所在的 TSU；题干 Nijay's university 指向 TSU，所以排除 H。\ngraduates just 16 percent of its first-generation students", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Some top institutions like Yale seem to provide first-generation students with more support than they actually need.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Yale 给的支持超过实际需要\n【定位】There is a lot of support at Yale, to an extent, after a while, there is too much support.\n耶鲁的支持很多，某种程度上，时间久了，支持甚至多到过头了。\n【改写】Yale ↔ Yale\n专名原样复现\nmore support than they actually need ↔ too much support\n比较句 ↔ too 程度副词\n【辨邻】 O O 段确实讲耶鲁的导师与教授提供支持，但落点是 lacked confidence about\n\"belonging\" 的信心建设，没有讲“too much / 过剩”；陈述句 Q39 的核心判断在 N\n段的 too much support，O 不承载这一句。\nprovided significant support for students who lacked confidence about \"belonging\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "On entering college, Nijay Williams had no idea how challenging college education was.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Nijay 入学时不知大学多难\n【定位】When Nijay Williams entered college last fall as a first-generation student and Jamaican\nimmigrant, he was academically unprepared for the rigors of higher education.\n去年秋天 Nijay Williams 作为第一代大学生和牙买加移民进入大学时，他在学业上还未准备\n好应对高等教育的严酷挑战。\n【改写】On entering college, Nijay Williams ↔ When Nijay Williams entered college last fall\n时点完全对位\nno idea how challenging college education was ↔\nacademically unprepared for the rigors of higher education\n认知空白 ↔ 准备不足\n【辨邻】 B B 段写 At the end of his first year, Nijay lost his Pell Grant 是 Nijay 第一年读完之\n后的事，不是题干 On entering college 的入学时点；时点错位，排除。\nAt the end of his first year, Nijay lost his Pell Grant of over $5,000", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Many universities simply refuse to release their exact graduation rates for first-generation students.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】大学拒绝公布第一代毕业率\n【定位】Many large institutions keep this kind of data secret—or at least make it incredibly difficult\nto find.\n许多大型院校把这类数据保密——或者至少让人极难找到。\n【改写】refuse to release their exact graduation rates ↔ keep this kind of data secret\n拒绝公布 ↔ 保密\nfor first-generation students ↔ graduation rate for its first-generation pupils\n学生群体限定相同\n【辨邻】 J J 段写 Higher education institutions are, under federal law, required to report\ngraduation rates, but these reports typically only include Pell recipient numbers\n——这是法规下报表口径不细，不是大学 refuse to release；归因不同，排除。\nthese reports typically only include Pell recipient numbers—not necessarily rates specific to first-\ngeneration students", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "According to a marketing executive, many students from low-income families don't know they could have a chance of going to an elite university.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】marketing executive 谈低收入家庭学生\n【定位】They underestimate themselves when selecting a university, said Dave Jarrat, a marketing\nexecutive for Inside Track, a for-profit organization that specializes in coaching low-income\nstudents and supporting colleges in order to help students thrive. The reality of it is that a lot\nof low-income kids could be going to elite universities on a full ride scholarship and don't\neven realize it.\nInside Track 的营销主管 Dave Jarrat 说：“他们在选大学时低估了自己。”——Inside Track\n是一家专门辅导低收入学生、支持大学的营利性组织。“实际情况是，许多低收入家庭的孩\n子完全可以拿全额奖学金进入精英大学，可他们自己都不知道这件事。”\n【改写】a marketing executive ↔ a marketing executive\n身份原样复现\nstudents from low-income families ↔ low-income kids\n家庭视角 ↔ 孩子视角\ndon't know they could have a chance of going to an elite university ↔\ncould be going to elite universities on a full ride scholarship and don't even realize it\ndon't know ↔ don't even realize\n【辨邻】 E E 段是 Rubinoff 在说 a lack of information and support to help students think\nbigger and broader——观点接近但说话人是非营利组织负责人，不是题干指定的“营\n销主管”；这是六级常见的“张冠李戴”陷阱，排除。\nthere tends to be a lack of information and support to help students think bigger and broader", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Some elite universities attach great importance to building up the first-generation students' self-confidence.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】精英校重视建立自信\n【定位】Our support structure was more like: 'You are going to get through Yale; you are going to do\nwell,' he said, hinting at mentors, staff, and professors who all provided significant support\nfor students who lacked confidence about 'belonging' at such a top institution.\n他说：“我们的支持氛围更像是'你能读完耶鲁，你会做得很好'”，暗指导师、职员和教授都给\n那些对自己能否归属顶尖名校缺乏信心的学生提供了大量支持。\n【改写】Some elite universities ↔ such a top institution\n精英校 ↔ 顶尖学府\nbuilding up the first-generation students' self-confidence ↔\nprovided significant support for students who lacked confidence about \"belonging\"\n缺自信 + 提供支持 = 建立自信\n【辨邻】 N N 段列举 small groups with counselors、cultural and ethnic affinity groups、\ntutoring centers 等支持种类，是“支持有什么”，而非“建立自信”；题干判断词是“self-\nconfidence”，落点必须是 O 段。\nthey have access to cultural and ethnic affinity groups, tutoring centers and also have a summer\norientation specifically for first-generation students\nM M 段写精英校 robust financial aid packages and remarkably high graduation\nrates for first-generation students——是奖学金与毕业率维度的支持，不是题干\nself-confidence 的自信维度。\nmore elite universities, which are often known for robust financial aid packages", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "I'm First distributes information to help first-generation college-goers find schools that are most suitable for them.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】I'm First 分发信息找合适学校\n【定位】He hopes to distribute this information and help prospective college-goers find the best post-\nsecondary fit.\n他希望分发这些信息，帮助未来的大学生找到最契合自己的大学。\n【改写】I'm First distributes information ↔ distribute this information\n动词宾语原样复现\nhelp first-generation college-goers find schools that are most suitable for them ↔\nhelp prospective college-goers find the best post-secondary fit\nmost suitable ↔ best fit\n【辨邻】 K K 段虽然讲 the launch of I'm First in 2013 与其目标 raise the success rate，但没\n有把“分发信息”作为动作明示；题干 distributes information 的核心动作只对应 D 段\ndistribute this information。\nIf we can help to direct students to more of these types of campuses", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Elite universities tend to graduate first-generation students at a higher rate.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】精英校第一代毕业率更高\n【定位】Still, Jones represents a small percentage of first-generation students who are able to gain\nentry into more elite universities, which are often known for robust financial aid packages\nand remarkably high graduation rates for first-generation students.\n尽管如此，Jones 仅是少数能进入更精英大学的第一代学生之一——这些精英大学常以丰厚\n的奖学金与极高的第一代学生毕业率著称。\n【改写】Elite universities ↔ more elite universities\n精英校直接对位\ngraduate first-generation students at a higher rate ↔\nremarkably high graduation rates for first-generation students\n比较级 ↔ remarkably high\n【辨邻】 H H 段 graduates just 16 percent of its first-generation students 与 39 percent 都\n是低毕业率的例子，与题干 at a higher rate 方向相反；同词反义，排除。\ngraduates just 16 percent of its first-generation students\nB B 段 a mere 29 percent 是 TSU 第一代学生毕业率，明显低，方向与 higher rate 相\n反；排除。\nfrighteningly low graduation rate a mere 29 percent for its first-generation students\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 讲第一代大学生 Nijay Williams 入学时学业上没准备好——他是 Jamaican\nimmigrant，进入一所中型州立大学，靠 Pell 助学金加联邦贷款，住家里且每周打工\n30-40 小时还要修满学分。\n信息指纹 Nijay 入学画像\nB\n揭出 Nijay 所在学校 Tennessee State University 第一代学生的毕业率仅 a mere 29\npercent，且 Nijay 因 GPA 差一点没到 2.0 而失去 Pell 助学金。\n信息指纹 TSU 29% 毕业率\nC\n把 Nijay 个案推广到群体：第一代大学生这一群入学时没准备好且学校也没能力让他\n们毕业；他们 carry financial burdens that outweigh those of their peers，更可能边读\n边打工，且常需补习。\n信息指纹 群体三重困境\nD\n介绍非营利组织 I'm First 及其负责人 Matt Rubinoff：组织于去年十月成立，专门面向\n第一代学生，distribute this information and help prospective college-goers find the\nbest post-secondary fit。\n信息指纹 I'm First 与 Rubinoff\nE Rubinoff 进一步说，第一代学生多数倾向于 online programs, two-year colleges, and\ncommuter state schools，存在 a lack of information and support to help students\nthink bigger and broader。\n信息指纹 Rubinoff 谈信息缺失\nF 尽管存在上述问题，许多学生仍倾向于熟悉、便宜的选项，尤其是两年制学校；作者\n作为前高中老师亲见他们年复一年选 community colleges or state schools with low\nbars for admittance。\n信息指纹 学生自选低门槛校\nG\n营销主管 Dave Jarrat 说低收入学生选校时低估自己，a lot of low-income kids could\nbe going to elite universities on a full ride scholarship and don't even realize it。\n信息指纹 Jarrat 谈自我低估\nH Jarrat 继续解释第一代学生 coming in questioning themselves and their college\nworthiness；并以田纳西大学 Knoxville 校区作例：整体毕业率 71%，第一代学生\n仅 16%。\n信息指纹 自我怀疑 + UT 例\nI\n夸 UT 透明的同时点出问题：许多大型机构对数据 keep this kind of data secret—or at\nleast make it incredibly difficult to find，以 UNC Chapel Hill 只说 much lower 为例。\n信息指纹 藏数据 vs 透明\nJ\n进一步说明数据不可得：联邦法律要求公布毕业率但通常只含 Pell recipient\nnumbers，不一定细分到第一代学生，普通家长学生很难看懂。\n信息指纹 联邦报表的盲区\nK 回到 I'm First 的成立缘起（2013 年），Rubinoff 阐述其目标：把学生引向更合适的校\n园、提高升学与毕业成功率。\n信息指纹 I'm First 成立缘起\nL I'm First 学生项目主管 Chelsea Jones 自述：高中 wasn't really a college-bound\nculture，靠 Princeton 的升学项目了解大学，但到 Howard 才发现 it was a completely\ndifferent ball game。\n信息指纹 Chelsea Jones 自述\nM\nHoward 给第一代学生提供配套（咨询、同伴、TRIO 项目）；但 Jones 属少数能进精\n英校的人——精英校往往奖学金丰厚、第一代学生毕业率极高（如哈佛 six-year\ngraduation rate for underrepresented minority groups of 98 percent）。\n信息指纹 精英校高毕业率\nN 另一例外是耶鲁第一代毕业生 Christian Vazquez，他半开玩笑说耶鲁的支持多到 after\na while, there is too much support，并列举各种配套（咨询小组、亲缘群体、辅导中\n心、迎新等）。\n信息指纹 Yale 支持过剩\nO Vazquez 进一步描述耶鲁的支持氛围 You are going to get through Yale; you are\ngoing to do well，强调导师 / 职员 / 教授帮助那些对 belonging 缺乏信心的学生。\n信息指纹 Yale 帮建归属感", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do some most influential medical groups recommend doctors do?", "options": ["A) Reflect on the responsibilities they are supposed to take.", "B) Pay more attention to the effectiveness of their treatments.", "C) Take costs into account when making treatment decisions.", "D) Readjust their practice in view of the cuts in health care."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do some most influential medical groups recommend doctors do? 是\nWhat-do 型直接事实问句，无 infer/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨信号，标准细\n节题。\n【拆句】主干是 What do some most influential medical groups recommend doctors do?：\nrecommend 后接\"宾语 + do\"省略 to 的兼语结构，问医疗组织推荐医生去做什么。结构平\n铺、无陷阱。\n【定位】Saying they can no longer ignore the rising price of health care, some of the most influential\nmedical groups in the nation are recommending that doctors weigh the costs, not just the\neffectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about patient care.\n由于无法再忽视医疗费用的不断上涨，美国一些最具影响力的医学组织正在建议医生在为病\n人做治疗决策时，不仅要考虑疗效，还要权衡成本。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nReflect on the responsibilities they are supposed to take 谈的是医生反思自身职\n责，定位句只说医学组织建议医生在决策中权衡成本，没有提及\"反思应承担的职责\"这\n层意思。\nB) 与原文相反\nPay more attention to the effectiveness of their treatments 让医生\"更关注疗效\"；\n而原文 not just the effectiveness of treatments 恰恰是要医生不要只盯疗效，要加上\n成本，方向相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndoctors weigh the costs, not just the effectiveness of treatments, as they make decisions about\npatient care\n↔ Take costs into account when making treatment decisions\n选项 C 同时承载\"成本\"与\"治疗决策\"两个语义点，且无任何超出原文的信息，是定位句\n的标准同义改写。\nD) 无中生有\nReadjust their practice in view of the cuts in health care 提到 cuts in health care，\n但原文谈的是 the rising price of health care，是涨价不是削减，且原文也没有要求医\n生\"重新调整执业\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What were doctors mainly concerned about in the past?", "options": ["A) Specific medicines to be used.", "B) Effects of medical treatment.", "C) Professional advancement.", "D) Patients' trust."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What were doctors mainly concerned about in the past? 用 What were 直接询问\n过去事实，没有 infer/imply 等信号，是定向找事实的细节题。\n【拆句】主干是 What were doctors mainly concerned about in the past?：mainly 修饰\nconcerned，in the past 是时间状语，问过去医生主要关心什么。介词 about 的宾语前置\n成 What，无陷阱。\n【定位】The shift, little noticed outside the medical establishment but already controversial inside it,\nsuggests that doctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively\nabout individual patients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent.\n这一转变在医疗界外鲜有人注意，但在医疗界内已颇具争议，它表明医生开始重新定义自己\n的角色——从过去只关注个体病人，转向影响医疗费用的去向。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nSpecific medicines to be used 把过去关注点缩到具体药物上；但原文说的是 being\nconcerned exclusively about individual patients，关心病人整体治疗远比\"用什么\n药\"宽泛，把整体偷换成局部。\nB) ✓ 正确\nbeing concerned exclusively about individual patients ↔ Effects of medical treatment\nthe effectiveness of treatments ↔ Effects of medical treatment\n选项 B 抓住\"过去只关心病人/疗效\"这一 from 后状态，且与首段 effectiveness of\ntreatments 互相印证。\nC) 无中生有\nProfessional advancement 谈医生的职业发展，全文没有任何一句涉及医生晋升、职\n级、收入等议题。\nD) 张冠李戴\nPatients' trust 在原文出现于 doctors risked losing the trust of patients，是医生承\n担双重角色的风险（属 Q49 的考点），不是过去关心的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What may the new guidelines being developed lead to?", "options": ["A) The redefining of doctors' roles.", "B) Overuse of less effective medicines.", "C) Conflicts between doctors and patients.", "D) The prolonging of patients' suffering."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What may the new guidelines being developed lead to? 直接询问指南导致什么结\n果，是事实型细节题，不是推断题，因为原文有 \"result in\" 直陈结果。\n【拆句】主干是 What may the new guidelines being developed lead to?：主语为 the new\nguidelines being developed，being developed 是进行时被动作后置定语修饰\nguidelines，谓语 may lead to，宾语前置为 What。结构稍密，但语义清楚。\n【定位】In practical terms, the new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one\ndrug over another for cost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment—at the end of\nlife, for example—is too expensive.\n实际而言，正在制定中的新指南可能导致医生因成本原因在两种药之间作选择，甚至判定某\n种治疗（例如临终治疗）过于昂贵。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndoctors are starting to redefine their roles, from being concerned exclusively about individual\npatients to exerting influence on how healthcare dollars are spent\n↔ The redefining of doctors' roles\nthe new guidelines being developed could result in doctors choosing one drug over another for\ncost reasons or even deciding that a particular treatment \\.\\.\\. is too expensive\n↔ The redefining of doctors' roles\n第 3 段所列后果在第 2 段已被上位概括为\"医生角色重定义\"，选项 A 正是这一上位\n归纳。\nB) 无中生有\\+程度词偷改\nOveruse of less effective medicines 谈\"过度使用效果更差的药\"；但原文 choosing\none drug over another for cost reasons 只说医生因成本选择，不必然导致\"过\n度\"或\"低效\"；且 P6 还明说两药 about equally effective，并非低效。\nC) 张冠李戴\nConflicts between doctors and patients 说医生与病人之间的冲突；原文谈的是医生\n群体内部对成本介入的争议（controversial inside it、P5、P8），并未把冲突对象指向\n病人。\nD) 与原文相反\nThe prolonging of patients' suffering 说延长病人痛苦；原文 deciding that a\nparticular treatment—at the end of life, for example—is too expensive 暗示的是\n放弃这种治疗（缩短而非延长），且原文未给\"病人痛苦\"做价值判断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What risk do doctors see in their dual role as patient care providers and financial overseers?", "options": ["A) They may be involved in a conflict of interest.", "B) They may be forced to divide their attention.", "C) They may have to use less effective drugs.", "D) They may lose the respect of patients."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What risk do doctors see in their dual role \\.\\.\\.? 直接问医生看到什么风险，是定\n向找事实的细节题。\n【拆句】主干是 What risk do doctors see in their dual role as patient care providers and\nfinancial overseers?：as 引导的介词短语作 dual role 的同位说明，列出双重身份两端；\nrisk 是 see 的前置宾语。结构平铺。\n【定位】He said doctors risked losing the trust of patients if they told patients, \"I'm not going\nto do what I think is best for you because I think it's bad for the healthcare budget\nin Massachusetts.\"\n他说，如果医生对病人说\"我不会做我认为对你最有利的事，因为我觉得这对马萨诸塞州的\n医疗预算不好\"，医生就有可能失去病人的信任。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey may be involved in a conflict of interest 谈\"利益冲突\"——通常指医生与药企/\n保险公司之间的经济利益冲突；原文 P5 谈的是医生职能定位与病人信任的张力，\n与\"conflict of interest\"这一专业概念不是一回事。\nB) 无中生有\nThey may be forced to divide their attention 说医生被迫分散注意力；原文只说担心\n同时承担两个角色会损害病人信任，没有谈\"注意力被迫分散\"。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey may have to use less effective drugs 说医生不得不用低效药；原文中关于药物\n的讨论在 P6 P7，且原文说两药 about equally effective，并不存在\"低效\"，更与 Q49\n问的\"双重身份风险\"不在同一区间。\nD) ✓ 正确\ndoctors risked losing the trust of patients ↔ They may lose the respect of patients\n选项 D 同时承载\"风险\"与\"失去病人正向评价\"两个语义点，与定位句吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do some experts say about doctors' involvement in medical cost analysis?", "options": ["A) It may add to doctors' already heavy workloads.", "B) It will help to save money for society as a whole.", "C) It results from society's failure to tackle the problem.", "D) It raises doctors' awareness of their social responsibilities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do some experts say about doctors' involvement in medical cost\nanalysis? 直接问专家观点，原文有直接引语承载，是细节题而非推断题。\n【拆句】主干是 What do some experts say about doctors' involvement in medical cost\nanalysis?：about 后是名词化短语 doctors' involvement in medical cost analysis，\ndoctors' 是所有格作 involvement 的逻辑主语。结构清楚。\n【定位】Still, some analysts say that there's a role for doctors to play in cost analysis because not\nmany others are doing so. \"In some ways,\" said Dr. Daniel Sulmasy, \"it represents a failure of\nwider society to take up the issue.\"\n尽管如此，一些分析人士说，医生在成本分析中有自己的作用可发挥，因为别的人没几个在\n做这件事。Dr. Daniel Sulmasy 说：\"在某种意义上，这反映了更广泛的社会未能承担这一\n议题的失败。\"\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt may add to doctors' already heavy workloads 说会加重医生本已沉重的工作量；\n全文未提工作量、加班、压力等任何劳动强度议题。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt will help to save money for society as a whole 说会帮社会省钱；但原文 not\ntrying to save money for the insurance industry or society as a whole 中\"save\nmoney for society as a whole\"恰恰是 Dr Jensen 在否定的对象，且 Sulmasy 的话并\n未承诺\"省钱\"。把否定语境里的短语正向搬运。\nC) ✓ 正确\nit represents a failure of wider society to take up the issue ↔\nIt results from society's failure to tackle the problem\nbecause not many others are doing so ↔\nIt results from society's failure to tackle the problem\n选项 C 精准把原文\"代表/源于社会失败\"的因果关系搬到选项里，无遗漏无超出。\nD) 过度推断\nIt raises doctors' awareness of their social responsibilities 说提升医生的社会责任意\n识；Sulmasy 的话谈的是社会的失败导致医生不得不介入，并未谈医生意识被唤醒。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "How does Obama view economic inequality?", "options": ["A) It is the biggest obstacle to social mobility.", "B) It is the greatest threat to social stability.", "C) It is the No. 1 enemy of income growth.", "D) It is the most malicious social evil of our time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How does Obama view economic inequality? 直接问某人对某事的看法，属于「找\n观点」型细节题，必有原文对应句。\n【拆句】题干 How does Obama view economic inequality? 是 how 引导的特殊疑问句，主干是\nObama view economic inequality，view 在此作「看待」讲，是熟词僻义，提醒：不要把\n它读成「风景/视野」。\n【定位】Inequality is dangerous, he argued, not merely because it doesn't look good to have a large\ngap between the rich and the poor, but because inequality itself destroys upward mobility,\nmaking it harder for the poor to escape from poverty.\n他论证道，不平等之所以危险，并不仅仅因为贫富鸿沟难看，更因为不平等本身摧毁了向上\n流动，使穷人更难脱离贫困。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ninequality itself destroys upward mobility ↔ It is the biggest obstacle to social mobility.\nIncreased inequality and decreasing mobility pose a fundamental threat to the American Dream\n↔ the biggest obstacle\n预判的「阻碍向上流动」直接对应 biggest obstacle to social mobility，与原文\ndestroy upward mobility 同义。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈的是 upward mobility（向上流动）与 American Dream，从未将不平等与\nsocial stability（社会稳定）挂钩。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 declare inequality Public Enemy No.1 and the greatest threat to reducing\npoverty 的「头号公敌」针对的是 reducing poverty/social mobility，不是\nincome growth。\nD) 过度推断\nObama 只说不平等 dangerous 且 destroys upward mobility，并未做道德评判称之\n为 malicious social evil（恶毒的社会罪恶）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about the inequality gap from Scott Winship's data analysis?", "options": ["A) It is fast widening across most parts of America.", "B) It is not a reliable indicator of economic mobility.", "C) It is not correctly interpreted.", "D) It is overwhelmingly ignored."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about the inequality gap from Scott Winship's data analysis?\n问的是某人分析得出的具体结论，是定向找细节。\n【拆句】from 短语后置作状语，说明数据来源；题干主干是 What do we learn about the\ninequality gap，核心是 inequality gap 的属性。\n【定位】Inequality itself is not a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility, as sociologist\nScott Winship noted in a recent article based on his analysis of this data.\n正如社会学家 Scott Winship 在最近一篇基于该数据的分析文章中所指出，不平等本身并不\n是经济流动性的特别强的预测指标。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文未谈不平等差距在美国「是否正在迅速扩大」，Winship 的结论关心的是它的预测\n力，不是它本身的变化趋势。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnot a particularly strong predictor of economic mobility ↔\nIt is not a reliable indicator of economic mobility.\n把 predictor 认出为 indicator 的同义改写、并保留否定，not a reliable indicator 即\n与定位句完全对应。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是 Winship 给出结论，并非评价别人对差距的「解读」是否正确；从 we're vastly\nexaggerating the dangers 顶多得「夸大了危险」，与「未被正确解读」不等价。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说不平等被给予了大量关注 For all the new attention devoted to the 1\npercent，Winship 之意是「过度关注」，与「被压倒性忽视」语义相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Compared with Atlanta, metropolitan Salt Lake City is said to _______.", "options": ["A) have placed religious beliefs above party politics", "B) have bridged the gap between the rich and the poor", "C) offer poor children more chances to climb the social ladder", "D) suffer from higher levels of racial and economic segregation"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Compared with Atlanta, metropolitan Salt Lake City is said to _______ 是补全句\n型，要求选出 Salt Lake City 的具体属性，本质是定向找细节。\n【拆句】Compared with Atlanta 是过去分词短语作状语，逻辑主语是后面的 metropolitan Salt\nLake City；is said to 后省略动词原形，选项接续。注意 said to 是「据说/被认为」，提示\n要找原文的客观描述句。\n【定位】Chetty finds that communities like Salt Lake City, with high levels of two-parent families and\nreligiosity, are much more likely to see poor children get ahead than communities like\nAtlanta, with high levels of racial and economic segregation.\nChetty 发现：像 Salt Lake City 这样高两亲家庭与宗教度的社区，比 Atlanta 这样高种族\n与经济隔离的社区，要更有可能让贫困儿童出人头地。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只提及 Salt Lake City 高 religiosity（宗教度），未谈「将宗教置于党派政治之\n上」；party politics（党派政治）一词全文未出现。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说 Salt Lake City「更可能让穷孩子翻身」，并未说它已经 bridged the gap（弥\n合了贫富鸿沟）；从「流动性好」推不出「贫富差距已经消除」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmuch more likely to see poor children get ahead ↔\noffer poor children more chances to climb the social ladder\npoor children get ahead ↔ climb the social ladder\n定位比较句的主语 Salt Lake City 的属性正是 more likely to see poor children get\nahead，与 offer poor children more chances to climb the social ladder 完全\n同义。\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文明说 high levels of racial and economic segregation 形容的是 Atlanta，不是\nSalt Lake City；选项把对比关系颠倒。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is strongly correlated with social mobility according to economist Raj Chetty?", "options": ["A) Family structure.", "B) Racial equality.", "C) School education.", "D) Community density."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is strongly correlated with social mobility according to economist Raj\nChetty? 让考生在原文中找 Chetty 给出的最强相关因素，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】What 主语 + is strongly correlated with social mobility 是被动结构；according to 短\n语提示观点归属。注意 correlate with 是「与...相关」，不要误读成因果关系。\n【定位】Harvard economist Raj Chetty has pointed to economic and racial segregation, community\ndensity, the size of a community's middle class, the quality of schools, community religiosity,\nand family structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility.\"\n哈佛经济学家 Raj Chetty 指出经济与种族隔离、社区密度、中产规模、学校质量、社区宗\n教度，以及家庭结构——他将家庭结构称为「向上流动的单一最强相关因素」。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nfamily structure, which he calls the \"single strongest correlate of upward mobility\" ↔\nFamily structure.\n原文 family structure 被作者明文加引号封为「最强相关因素」，与 Family structure\n直接对接。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只提及 racial segregation（种族隔离）而非 racial equality（种族平等），二者并\n非同义；且 segregation 也未被 Chetty 提为最强相关项。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文 the quality of schools 只是六个并列因素之一，并未被 Chetty 单独标记为最强相\n关；选 C 是「以局部代最强」。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 community density 与 school 并列出现，未被任何最高级或独立从句强调；与 C\n错法同类。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author seem to suggest?", "options": ["A) It is important to increase the size of the middle class.", "B) It is highly important to expand the metropolitan areas.", "C) It is most imperative to focus our efforts on the elimination of income inequality.", "D) It is better to start from the community to help poor children move up the social ladder."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author seem to suggest? 问作者整体暗示，无关键词可定，需要把\n全文主线串起来。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author suggest 是宾语提问；seem to 弱化语气，提示作者并未\n明说，需考生从行文推断；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文 the size of a community's middle class 只是 Chetty 列出的六因素之一，并未被\n作者单独强调为最重要的政策抓手；且 P6 中作者挑出的三因素并不包含中产规模。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 metropolitan area 只是 Salt Lake City/Atlanta 的统称，不涉及「扩大都市区」\n的政策建议。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 we're vastly exaggerating the dangers of the rich-poor gap 已明白反驳「把消\n除收入不平等当首要任务」的看法；C 项恰是作者要反对的观点。\nD) ✓ 正确\ncommunities with high levels of per-capita income growth, high percentages of two-parent\nfamilies, and high local government spending...are the most likely to help poor children relive\nthe Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story\n↔ It is better to start from the community to help poor children move up the social ladder.\nthe Horatio Alger's rags-to-riches story ↔ move up the social ladder\n全文反驳「国家层面减不平等」主流观点，最终落到「社区层面措施」，D 项恰好覆盖\n这条主线。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
